(function(){ G.HFQ.addSeriesText("htfa-1",{"chapters":[{"t":"Prologue — The Sound Above the Field","blocks":[{"p":"The ball was already on its way down when I remembered I was supposed to catch it."},{"p":"I had my glove up. That was something. Unfortunately, I was looking at an airplane instead of the baseball, and the airplane was much too far away to catch."},{"p":"Somebody yelled my name. The ball struck the grass behind my left shoe and bounced toward the fence. I went after it, nearly lost my glove, and made a throw so wild our shortstop had to chase it into foul territory."},{"p":"By the time the runner crossed home plate, our center fielder had both hands on his head."},{"p":"The airplane kept going."},{"p":"It was small and white, with wings that flashed whenever it turned against the afternoon sun. I couldn’t hear the engine over the shouting. I watched it until it became a dot over the trees."},{"p":"Then Coach blew his whistle."},{"p":"“Burgess. Bench.”"},{"p":"I went."},{"p":"The dugout smelled like orange peels, damp dirt, and the sour leather of somebody’s old catcher’s mitt. I sat at the end of the bench and worked a pebble out of my cleat. Nobody said much to me. That was worse than getting yelled at."},{"p":"We lost by one run."},{"p":"Dad waited until we’d driven out of the parking lot before speaking. He had both hands on the wheel. The air conditioner blew straight at my knees because its middle vent was broken."},{"p":"“You know what happened out there?” he asked."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“You want to tell me?”"},{"p":"“Not especially.”"},{"p":"He nodded as though this were a reasonable answer. We passed a tire shop, a church sign advertising a fish fry, and a man on a bicycle carrying a ladder. I had no idea how he was managing that last one."},{"p":"“Airplane,” I said finally."},{"p":"Dad glanced at me. “An airplane?”"},{"p":"“I was looking at it.”"},{"p":"“I gathered you were looking at something.”"},{"p":"“It was going toward the coast.”"},{"p":"“Was it at least a good airplane?”"},{"p":"“What does that even mean?”"},{"p":"“If you’re going to lose a baseball game over an airplane, I’d like to think it was an exceptional one.”"},{"p":"I turned to the window. “I don’t know what kind it was.”"},{"p":"“That’s a shame.”"},{"p":"“You’re enjoying this.”"},{"p":"“A little.”"},{"p":"He reached over and nudged the air-conditioning vent away from my knees. It clicked back almost immediately."},{"p":"For the rest of the drive I tried to picture the airplane’s tail. One fin or two? I couldn’t remember. I could remember the way it banked, though, and how the sunlight slid along one wing before the whole thing disappeared behind a cloud."},{"p":"At home I put my glove in the garage without wiping off the dirt. I knew Dad would make me come back and do it. I went inside anyway."}]},{"t":"Chapter One — The Photograph","blocks":[{"p":"Uncle Everett believed a doorbell should be rung once."},{"p":"“If I’m home, I heard it,” he’d told me. “If I’m not, ringing again won’t help.”"},{"p":"Everett had flown fighter jets for the Navy, so people tended to let him have opinions about things."},{"p":"I rang once."},{"p":"Dad rang again before I could stop him."},{"p":"From inside came Everett’s voice. “One of you better be bleeding.”"},{"p":"Dad smiled. “Morning to you too.”"},{"p":"Everett opened the door wearing a faded polo shirt and a pair of jeans with a dark stain on one knee. He was tall but not quite as tall as Dad, with silver hair cut close to his head and a nose that bent slightly to one side. A pencil was tucked behind his ear."},{"p":"He looked at my baseball glove. “How’d the game go?”"},{"p":"“Fine.”"},{"p":"“We lost,” Dad said."},{"p":"“Close?”"},{"p":"“One run.”"},{"p":"Everett gave me a sympathetic look. “Tough one.”"},{"p":"“Your nephew had an excellent view of the decisive play,” Dad added. “From underneath it.”"},{"p":"I stepped past them into the hallway. “Do you have pancakes?”"},{"p":"“Kitchen,” Everett said. “And wash your hands.”"},{"p":"He lived alone in a narrow two-story house with a front yard so tidy it made ours look like a nature preserve. The house smelled of coffee and the soldering iron he used on old radios. Every time we visited, some electronic thing was spread across his dining-room table, its screws lined up in a little dish. Mom said he had too much time on his hands. Everett said retirement was supposed to work that way."},{"p":"I washed my hands, found the pancakes, and ate three while Dad and Everett argued about whether the kitchen faucet needed a new washer or an entirely new fixture. They were still arguing when I carried my plate to the sink."},{"p":"“Where’s the little radio?” I asked."},{"p":"“Study,” Everett said. “Don’t touch the copper coil. I just rewound it.”"},{"p":"I went down the hall."},{"p":"The radio sat on the desk with its back off, but it wasn’t what stopped me. Above the desk hung a photograph of an airplane on an aircraft carrier. I’d passed that picture plenty of times. Maybe it was yesterday’s baseball game, but I stood there studying it as if someone had changed the photograph overnight."},{"p":"The jet was long and gray, its wings swept back so sharply that it looked ready to move even while parked. Two tails rose behind the engines. A crew member stood beside its nose, tiny by comparison."},{"p":"“F-14D,” Everett said from behind me."},{"p":"I jumped. “You walk too quietly.”"},{"p":"“You were concentrating.”"},{"p":"“Did you fly that one?”"},{"p":"“I flew that type. I’d have to look up the aircraft number to tell you whether I flew the exact jet in the photograph.”"},{"p":"He stood beside me, one shoulder a little higher than the other. Mom said he’d injured it years ago. He never brought it up, even when somebody asked why he couldn’t throw a baseball properly."},{"p":"“That’s a Tomcat,” I said."},{"p":"“Yep.”"},{"p":"“Why are its wings like that?”"},{"p":"“They can sweep back or move forward. The angle changes for different parts of flight.”"},{"p":"“Did you move them yourself?”"},{"p":"“Usually a computer controlled the wing sweep automatically. The crew had other controls when needed.”"},{"p":"I leaned closer. A little black cat carrying a bomb was painted on a patch beneath the frame."},{"p":"“What’s that?”"},{"p":"“Felix. The Tomcatters. VF-31.”"},{"p":"“That’s a terrible name.”"},{"p":"“I’ll tell the squadron.”"},{"p":"“Can you?”"},{"p":"“No.” He rubbed his chin. “Not without making a few phone calls, anyway.”"},{"p":"I pointed to another photograph, a different fighter with fixed wings and a broad, blunt-looking nose. “And that one?”"},{"p":"“F/A-18E Super Hornet.”"},{"p":"“Which was better?”"},{"p":"Everett made a face. “At what?”"},{"p":"“Flying.”"},{"p":"“They both flew.”"},{"p":"“Uncle Everett.”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“Which did you like more?”"},{"p":"He took the photograph off the shelf, brushed dust from its edge with his thumb, and set it down again. “I had good days and bad days in both.”"},{"p":"“That isn’t an answer.”"},{"p":"“It’s the one you’ve got.”"},{"p":"He’d flown Tomcats with VF-31 and had been there when the squadron changed aircraft in 2006. I knew that much because Dad had told me, usually when Everett wasn’t around. I also knew Everett didn’t like people introducing him as a fighter pilot at restaurants."},{"p":"“Wasn’t it hard to learn a new jet?” I asked."},{"p":"“Of course.”"},{"p":"“But you already knew how to fly.”"},{"p":"He looked at me. “You already know how to play baseball, don’t you?”"},{"p":"My ears warmed."},{"p":"“That’s different.”"},{"p":"“Is it?”"},{"p":"I wished Dad hadn’t told him about the game. Then I remembered I’d practically announced it in the doorway."},{"p":"Everett went back to the desk and picked up a tiny screwdriver. He turned it between his fingers without looking at the radio."},{"p":"“Knowing how to do something isn’t the same as doing it well every time,” he said. “And knowing one airplane doesn’t mean you can step into another without training.”"},{"p":"“Did you ever get things wrong?”"},{"p":"“Frequently enough to remember the instructors who caught me.”"},{"p":"“Even when you were a commander?”"},{"p":"“Rank doesn’t make the runway wider.”"},{"p":"I thought about that. He didn’t seem interested in explaining further, so I asked the question that had been stuck in my head since yesterday."},{"p":"“Could I learn?”"},{"p":"“Learn what?”"},{"p":"“To fly.”"},{"p":"The screwdriver stopped turning."},{"p":"“Maybe,” he said."},{"p":"“Maybe?”"},{"p":"“You’re thirteen. You can start learning plenty now. If you want lessons in an airplane, you’ll need a qualified instructor, your parents’ agreement, money, and some patience.”"},{"p":"“I have patience.”"},{"p":"Everett looked at me for long enough that I got annoyed."},{"p":"“What?”"},{"p":"“Nothing.”"},{"p":"“You’re doing a face.”"},{"p":"“Am I?”"},{"p":"He set the screwdriver down and opened a drawer. There were batteries, a packet of seeds, several rubber bands, and a folded brochure. He gave me the brochure without saying anything."},{"p":"On the front was a small airplane parked beside a hangar. The words HYPE TEEN FLIGHT ACADEMY ran across the top."},{"p":"I opened it. There were pictures of students examining a chart, sitting at a simulator, and standing beside a high-wing airplane. I read the paragraph about introductory flights twice."},{"p":"“Where is this?”"},{"p":"“They meet over at Hampton Roads Executive Airport in Chesapeake. Youth program. They work with a civilian flight school for the flying side.”"},{"p":"“Can you take me?”"},{"p":"“Not my decision.”"},{"p":"“Can you ask Mom?”"},{"p":"“You can ask Mom.”"},{"p":"I folded the brochure so sharply it left a white crease across the airplane on the cover."},{"p":"Everett noticed. He held out his hand, and I gave it back. He flattened the paper against the desk with his palm."},{"p":"“Lincoln, this is the first thing you need to understand about flying. You don’t get the answer you want just because you want it badly.”"},{"p":"I stared at the brochure."},{"p":"“Sometimes,” he added, “you don’t get an answer at all until you’ve done the homework.”"},{"p":"From the kitchen Dad called, “Everett, you’ve got a pancake turning into a fossil in here.”"},{"p":"Everett tucked the brochure into my hand. “Come on.”"},{"p":"I stayed a second longer, looking at the Tomcat photograph. The little crew member stood beside the nose wheel, one hand raised to somebody outside the frame."},{"p":"I wanted to know what he’d been saying."}]},{"t":"Chapter Two — Numbers","blocks":[{"p":"By Sunday morning, the brochure had a grease mark from breakfast and a corner that refused to lie flat."},{"p":"Mom found me at the kitchen table with a pencil, three sheets of notebook paper, and a bowl of cereal I hadn’t touched."},{"p":"“Is that homework?” she asked."},{"p":"“Sort of.”"},{"p":"“School homework?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"She opened the dishwasher. A fork fell out of the silverware basket and hit the tile with a crack. Mom stared at it as though the fork had made a personal choice."},{"p":"“Then eat first.”"},{"p":"I pushed the brochure toward her. “Can I join?”"},{"p":"She read the cover. “This is the place Everett mentioned.”"},{"p":"“He told you?”"},{"p":"“He called yesterday.”"},{"p":"“What did he say?”"},{"p":"“That you’re interested in airplanes.”"},{"p":"“That’s all?”"},{"p":"“He also said to ask you about the baseball game.”"},{"p":"I took the brochure back."},{"p":"Mom retrieved the fork and dropped it into the sink. She worked for the city, handling records, forms, and people who got annoyed about both. She could find the missing page in a stack of paperwork faster than I could find my shoes. I wasn’t sure whether that made her qualified to judge flight schools, but she looked at the brochure the way she looked at everything that might cost money."},{"p":"“There’s no price,” she said."},{"p":"“They have scholarships.”"},{"p":"“Scholarships for what?”"},{"p":"“For the program.”"},{"p":"“How much of the program?”"},{"p":"“I don’t know.”"},{"p":"“How often does it meet?”"},{"p":"“I don’t know.”"},{"p":"“Then let’s find out before we make plans.”"},{"p":"“You always say that.”"},{"p":"“Usually because we don’t have the information.”"},{"p":"I scraped the spoon against the bowl. The cereal had turned into a wet, pale lump."},{"p":"Mom pulled out a chair and sat beside me. Her coffee smelled bitter. She didn’t reach for the brochure again."},{"p":"“I’m not trying to ruin it,” she said."},{"p":"“I didn’t say you were.”"},{"p":"“You were thinking it loudly.”"},{"p":"I looked at the table."},{"p":"“Your dad and I have to be careful with money, Lincoln. You know that.”"},{"p":"I did know. Our house was fine. My clothes fit. We went places in the summer, sometimes. But the washing machine had been making a banging noise for two months, Dad’s car had a window that wouldn’t close properly, and when I asked for a new game last week, Mom had said we’d talk after the electric bill came."},{"p":"“I could pay for some of it,” I said."},{"p":"“With what?”"},{"p":"“Lawns. Birthdays. I could sell stuff.”"},{"p":"“Don’t sell anything yet.”"},{"p":"“Why does everybody think I’m going to do something stupid?”"},{"p":"She stared at me over her coffee. “I didn’t say that.”"},{"p":"I knew she hadn’t. I kept staring at the table anyway."},{"p":"Mom stood, rinsed her mug, and put it upside down on the drying rack. “Find out what it costs. Then we’ll talk.”"},{"p":"“That’s not a yes.”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“It’s not a no either.”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"She went upstairs with a basket of towels. I sat listening to the washing machine thump through its spin cycle."},{"p":"I ate the soggy cereal. Leaving it there wouldn’t improve my chances."},{"brk":1},{"p":"Dad’s computer occupied a desk in the room off the hallway. The printer beside it had been broken so many times that Dad had named it Gerald. Nobody remembered why."},{"p":"I typed how much does it cost to become a pilot into the search bar."},{"p":"The first result was more than ten thousand dollars."},{"p":"I refreshed the page as if that might help."},{"p":"It didn’t."},{"p":"Some estimates were higher. A lot higher. They included airplane rental, instructor time, books, exams, and things I’d never heard of. I found the word checkride and pictured somebody checking whether your airplane was comfortable. That turned out to be wrong."},{"p":"I wrote the numbers on a sheet of paper. By the time Dad appeared in the doorway, I’d crossed out three calculations and drawn a box around a number that made my stomach hurt."},{"p":"“Everything all right?” he asked."},{"p":"“How much does a used car cost?”"},{"p":"“That depends on the car.”"},{"p":"“Like a normal one.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Because flying lessons cost more.”"},{"p":"Dad came over. His glasses slid down his nose as he read the screen. He was a librarian at the public library and could make almost any question into a research assignment. Usually I avoided asking him questions near a computer."},{"p":"“This is an estimate for a private pilot certificate,” he said."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Which isn’t what you asked your mother about.”"},{"p":"“It’s the same thing eventually.”"},{"p":"“Eventually is doing a lot of work in that sentence.”"},{"p":"I pointed at the number. “How am I supposed to save that much?”"},{"p":"Dad sat in the chair beside me. He didn’t say I’d find a way. He didn’t say dreams were worth the money. He read the page all the way to the bottom, including the small print."},{"p":"“First,” he said, “we need to know whether the academy costs anything close to this. Second, we need to understand what you’d actually be doing there.”"},{"p":"“I’d be flying.”"},{"p":"“Maybe. Or learning about flying. Or building models. We don’t know.”"},{"p":"“It’s in the brochure.”"},{"p":"“Some of it is. Do you know the schedule?”"},{"p":"I didn’t answer."},{"p":"“Do you know what the scholarship covers?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Then you’ve got a place to start.”"},{"p":"He opened a blank email. I watched him move the mouse, waiting for him to type."},{"p":"“Well?” I asked."},{"p":"“Well what?”"},{"p":"“Aren’t you going to write it?”"},{"p":"“It’s your question.”"},{"p":"“I’m thirteen.”"},{"p":"“I know. You’ve mentioned it.”"},{"p":"I pulled the keyboard toward me and wrote: Hi I want to fly airplanes."},{"p":"Dad read it over my shoulder. “Accurate.”"},{"p":"I erased it."},{"p":"It took me twenty minutes to write six sentences. I asked about cost, meeting times, scholarships, and whether a thirteen-year-old could take an introductory flight with an instructor. I made Dad read it twice. He changed one comma and told me to stop worrying about the greeting."},{"p":"Then I hit Send."},{"p":"Nothing happened, except the message disappeared."},{"p":"I checked my inbox."},{"p":"Still nothing."},{"p":"Dad got up. “They’re probably not working today.”"},{"p":"I stared at him. “You knew that?”"},{"p":"“It’s Sunday.”"},{"p":"“You could’ve told me.”"},{"p":"“Would you have written the email?”"},{"p":"“Probably.”"},{"p":"“Then I saved us a conversation.”"},{"p":"He went to fold laundry. I refreshed my inbox again, because it was possible someone at the academy loved working Sundays."},{"brk":1},{"p":"In the garage that afternoon, Dad was untangling extension cords from a plastic bin. I went out to ask whether we could afford one flight, just one, if the academy didn’t offer scholarships."},{"p":"Before I could speak, he handed me a cord."},{"p":"“Hold that end.”"},{"p":"I held it. He pulled the other end and tightened the knot around my wrist."},{"p":"“Dad.”"},{"p":"“Sorry.” He backed up and loosened it."},{"p":"I watched him work. There were tools on the bench, a dented coffee can full of screws, and a cardboard box labeled CHRISTMAS LIGHTS that hadn’t contained Christmas lights in years."},{"p":"“What if I do all this,” I said, “and I still can’t go?”"},{"p":"Dad stopped pulling the cord."},{"p":"“Do what?”"},{"p":"“Read stuff. Ask questions. Save money. What if we still can’t pay for it?”"},{"p":"He set the cord down and rubbed the bridge of his nose where his glasses had left two red marks."},{"p":"“That could happen.”"},{"p":"I stared at him. “You’re supposed to say something else.”"},{"p":"“Like what?”"},{"p":"“I don’t know. That it’ll work out.”"},{"p":"“I hope it does.”"},{"p":"“That’s not the same.”"},{"p":"“No,” he said. “It isn’t.”"},{"p":"From the driveway came the sound of a car door closing. Mom had gone to get groceries. Dad picked up the cord again, wound it around his elbow, and hung it on a hook."},{"p":"“I can promise we’ll look into it,” he said. “I can promise we’ll take it seriously. I can’t promise we can buy you an airplane ride whenever you want one.”"},{"p":"“I don’t want one whenever I want.”"},{"p":"He glanced at me."},{"p":"“Okay,” I admitted. “I kind of do.”"},{"p":"Dad handed me another cord. This one was tangled around a string of lights. I worked the plug through a loop, then another, until the whole knot collapsed in my hands."},{"p":"“Hey,” Dad said. “There you go.”"},{"p":"I hung it on the hook without answering."}]},{"t":"Chapter Three — The Yellow Line","blocks":[{"p":"The email arrived Tuesday at 10:14 a.m., during a fractions quiz."},{"p":"I knew the time because I checked my phone under the desk. Mistake. The screen lit up my left knee like a tiny interrogation lamp."},{"p":"“Lincoln,” Mrs. Fenn said."},{"p":"I slid the phone into my pocket."},{"p":"“Are you solving the problem or consulting somebody who is?”"},{"p":"“Solving.”"},{"p":"“Then I’d love to see some numbers on your paper.”"},{"p":"My paper contained one number, and it wasn’t a fraction. I wrote down another to keep it company."},{"p":"By the time the bell rang, I’d nearly chewed through the end of my pencil. The message was from Hype Teen Flight Academy. It listed a Saturday orientation at Hampton Roads Executive Airport, a parent information meeting, and the fee for the introductory program. There were scholarships, but they were limited. No flight was included in the orientation."},{"p":"That last sentence disappointed me so much I read it three times, as if the words might rearrange themselves."},{"p":"Mom read the email at dinner. Dad asked whether we could attend without committing to anything. We could. Mom said she wanted to meet the people responsible for the program before we discussed money. Dad agreed, then asked me to pass the peas."},{"p":"I passed them without complaining. This was probably the most mature thing I’d done all week."},{"brk":1},{"p":"Saturday smelled like hot pavement and somebody’s lawn being cut two streets over. Dad drove. Mom sat beside him with a printed copy of the email folded into quarters. I sat in the back with my notebook, trying to read a diagram of an airplane wing while the car bounced over every crack in the road."},{"p":"At the airport, the buildings were lower than I’d imagined. I don’t know why I’d expected towers of glass. There were hangars, parked airplanes, a few cars, and an ordinary-looking office with a blue awning. A windsock stretched away from its pole, sagged, then filled again."},{"p":"“Is that the wind direction?” I asked."},{"p":"“Generally,” Dad said."},{"p":"“What do you mean, generally?”"},{"p":"“Ask somebody who knows.”"},{"p":"I wrote WINDSOCK in my notebook, underlined it, and climbed out."},{"p":"A yellow line ran along the pavement outside the building. Beyond it stood a chain-link fence and a gate. An airplane taxied past on the other side, its propeller a pale blur. I felt the engine’s vibration through the pavement."},{"p":"I walked toward the gate."},{"p":"“Stop there.”"},{"p":"The voice wasn’t loud. I stopped anyway."},{"p":"A woman in a gray polo shirt stood beside the office door, holding a clipboard. She was tall, with dark-blond hair pulled back low and a small dark mole near her chin. Her sunglasses hung from the collar of her shirt."},{"p":"“Yellow line,” she said, pointing down. “Visitors stay on this side unless an authorized staff member escorts them.”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t going through the gate.”"},{"p":"“You were headed toward it.”"},{"p":"“I just wanted to see the plane.”"},{"p":"“So do most people who come here.” She looked at my parents. “Mara Ellery. I’m one of the instructors working with the youth program.”"},{"p":"Mom shook her hand. Dad did too. I kept looking at the airplane until it disappeared behind a hangar."},{"p":"Mara waited until I faced her."},{"p":"“What’s your name?”"},{"p":"“Lincoln Burgess.”"},{"p":"“First time at an airport this close to the airplanes?”"},{"p":"“First time at this one.”"},{"p":"She seemed to hear the distinction I’d made, though she didn’t comment on it."},{"p":"“Good. Let’s get you signed in.”"},{"p":"Inside, the air conditioner hummed hard enough to rattle a vent. A folding table held name tags, paper cups, and a plate of cookies that had already been attacked. Three other kids stood near a wall covered with photographs."},{"p":"One of them had sandy-blond hair cut short and square glasses that kept sliding down his nose. He was turning a plastic model airplane upside down, studying its landing gear."},{"p":"“That’s not the right wheel assembly,” he said to nobody in particular."},{"p":"A girl with neat black braids looked over his shoulder. “It’s a toy.”"},{"p":"“It’s still wrong.”"},{"p":"“Then write the toy company a letter.”"},{"p":"The third kid, taller than both of them, had dark curls and an easy grin. “Please do. I want to read their response.”"},{"p":"I laughed. All three looked at me."},{"p":"“I’m Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“Beckett,” said the boy with the model. “That’s Imani. He’s Rafe.”"},{"p":"“Rafael,” Rafe said. “Unless I’m in trouble.”"},{"p":"“Are you usually?” I asked."},{"p":"“Depends who’s keeping score.”"},{"p":"Mara called us to the chairs. Our parents settled behind us. She wrote three words on a whiteboard: LOOK. ASK. LISTEN."},{"p":"“Before we talk about flying,” she said, “tell me why those belong together.”"},{"p":"Rafe raised his hand. “You look for traffic, talk on the radio, and listen for other pilots.”"},{"p":"“Good start,” Mara said. “There’s no control tower here to clear us for taxi. Pilots watch for other traffic, use the radio to share their intentions, and follow the airport’s procedures. But that’s not what I mean.”"},{"p":"I raised mine. “You look at the instruments, ask what they mean, and listen to the instructor.”"},{"p":"“Closer.”"},{"p":"Beckett didn’t raise his hand. “You check the airplane before you touch anything.”"},{"p":"Mara tapped the board with the marker. “That’s a good place to start.”"},{"p":"I sat straighter. I’d expected a cockpit, maybe a demonstration of the controls. Instead we were discussing three words that could have come from a classroom poster."},{"p":"I looked past Mara through the window. A high-wing airplane was being pushed out of a hangar by two people, one at each wing strut."},{"p":"“Lincoln?”"},{"p":"I turned back."},{"p":"“What was the last thing I said?”"},{"p":"There was a pause just long enough for everyone to know."},{"p":"“About checking the airplane?”"},{"p":"“No. I said that noticing something isn’t the same as understanding it.” Mara put the cap on her marker. “It’s all right to miss a sentence. It isn’t all right to pretend you heard it.”"},{"p":"My ears got hot. Behind me, Dad shifted in his chair. I kept my eyes on the whiteboard for the rest of the talk."}]},{"t":"Chapter Four — The Airplane on the Other Side","blocks":[{"p":"After the presentation, Mara took our group outside with another staff member and led us along the marked pedestrian route. She counted us twice, once at the door and again by the gate. Nobody complained. I noticed because I wanted to complain, but couldn’t find a good reason."},{"p":"Up close, the airplane looked smaller than it had from the office window. It was a Cessna 172S, white with a narrow dark stripe along its side. The wings stretched over our heads. I could see tiny scratches in the paint near the door handle and dried insects on the leading edge."},{"p":"“This is the kind of airplane some of you may eventually train in,” Mara said. “Today we’re looking. No climbing in, no touching the propeller, no switches.”"},{"p":"Beckett stepped sideways to see the nosewheel. “Is that a shimmy damper?”"},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"He nodded as if somebody had confirmed a suspicion about a neighbor."},{"p":"Imani was watching the windsock. “It changed.”"},{"p":"“What did?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“The wind. It was pointing more toward the hangars earlier.”"},{"p":"Mara looked across the ramp. “Good catch. What else can you use to get information about the wind?”"},{"p":"“Airport weather reports,” I said quickly."},{"p":"“That’s one source.”"},{"p":"I liked the way she said it. It meant I had an answer."},{"p":"Rafe pointed at the tail. “Can you tell the wind from which way airplanes park?”"},{"p":"“Not reliably,” Mara said. “Airplanes are parked where people put them.”"},{"p":"“Worth a try.”"},{"p":"“Absolutely.”"},{"p":"Mara showed us the pitot tube, the static port, and the hinged control surfaces. She didn’t explain every part. When Beckett asked about a small fitting beneath the wing, she crouched to look at it instead of guessing."},{"p":"“Good question,” she said. “I’ll check this airplane’s documentation before I tell you what that fitting is.”"},{"p":"Beckett looked pleased. I wasn’t sure why. She’d just admitted she didn’t know."},{"p":"At the left wing, Mara asked us what we noticed."},{"p":"“Flaps,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Aileron,” I said at the same time."},{"p":"We both pointed. To different parts."},{"p":"“Both are here,” Mara said. “Which one did you mean, Lincoln?”"},{"p":"I kept my finger extended toward the inner trailing edge. Beckett’s finger pointed farther out."},{"p":"For a second I considered moving mine."},{"p":"“Flap,” I said."},{"p":"“Right.”"},{"p":"“I knew that.”"},{"p":"Beckett glanced at me. “Then why’d you say aileron?”"},{"p":"“Because I was talking.”"},{"p":"He pushed his glasses up. “That’s usually when people say things.”"},{"p":"Rafe made a strangled noise. Imani turned away, but I could see her smiling."},{"p":"Mara stepped between us without seeming to. “The important part is that you corrected it before anyone used the wrong information.”"},{"p":"I hadn’t corrected anything. I’d been caught. But my parents were a few yards away, and I wasn’t about to explain that in front of them."},{"p":"We moved to the tail. The horizontal stabilizer was above my head, the elevator hanging from its trailing edge. Mara talked about pitch and asked us to imagine the nose rising and lowering. I could follow that. The rudder was easy enough, too."},{"p":"Then she asked why a pilot might need to coordinate the controls in a turn."},{"p":"I started to answer. Stopped. Beckett was looking at me, waiting."},{"p":"“Because turning isn’t just steering,” I said."},{"p":"Mara nodded. “Keep going.”"},{"p":"I couldn’t."},{"p":"The wind stirred the hair on my forehead. Somewhere behind us a metal hangar door rattled along its track."},{"p":"“I don’t know the rest.”"},{"p":"“Good place to begin,” she said, and this time it didn’t sound like a compliment. It sounded like a fact."},{"p":"At the end of orientation, Mara handed each of us a single sheet with the next month’s meeting dates. Any flights would be arranged separately with the partner flight school. We’d need a parent’s permission, an available instructor and airplane, and a way to pay for it. Nobody promised us a flight that day."},{"p":"I was reading the sheet when Beckett came over."},{"p":"“You were right about the rudder,” he said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“And the flap was easy to mix up from that angle.”"},{"p":"I folded the sheet in half. “Are you trying to make me feel better?”"},{"p":"“No. I’m saying it was easy to mix up.”"},{"p":"“Right.”"},{"p":"He watched me for another second. “You don’t have to be mad.”"},{"p":"“I’m not mad.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"He walked away. I hated how calm he was about it."},{"p":"Dad found me beside the water fountain. He had a cookie in one hand and a paper packet in the other."},{"p":"“Well?” he asked."},{"p":"“It’s good.”"},{"p":"“Just good?”"},{"p":"“Really good.”"},{"p":"He held out the packet. “Your mom’s talking with the program coordinator about the fee assistance application.”"},{"p":"I took it. Three pages. There were boxes for household information, program interests, and a short statement from the student."},{"p":"The last question read: *What do you hope to learn, and why is this opportunity important to you?*"},{"p":"I stared at the empty lines."},{"p":"Dad took a bite of cookie. “You can work on that at home.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“You’ve got time.”"},{"p":"I rubbed my thumb along the fold in the paper until it softened."},{"p":"Across the ramp, Mara was walking with a mechanic toward the airplane. They stopped by the left wing and bent to look at something beneath it. Neither seemed in a hurry."},{"p":"I watched them for a while, then turned the packet over and tried to think of an answer that didn’t begin with *I want to fly because flying is cool*."}]},{"t":"Chapter Five — The Part Nobody Photographs","blocks":[{"p":"The scholarship question sat on our kitchen table for four days."},{"p":"It moved around. Mom shifted it away from a bowl of peaches. Dad rescued it from a puddle left by his water glass. I carried it upstairs once, wrote my name at the top, and brought it down again because I couldn’t think of anything else to say."},{"p":"*What do you hope to learn, and why is this opportunity important to you?*"},{"p":"I hoped to learn to fly. It was important because I wanted to fly. The people who wrote the question clearly hadn’t considered how difficult they’d made it to answer honestly."},{"p":"Thursday evening, Dad found me drawing a box around the word *important*."},{"p":"“Planning to trap it?” he asked."},{"p":"“It’s a terrible question.”"},{"p":"“Could be. What’s terrible about it?”"},{"p":"“Everybody’s going to write the same thing.”"},{"p":"“Probably.” He pulled out the chair across from me. “So don’t.”"},{"p":"I pushed the paper toward him. “What would you write?”"},{"p":"“It’s not my application.”"},{"p":"“You could pretend.”"},{"p":"He studied the blank lines, then slid the page back. “I’d write that I wanted to know whether I liked flying enough to do the boring parts.”"},{"p":"“That’s a bad scholarship answer.”"},{"p":"“Maybe. It’s a good question, though.”"},{"p":"I didn’t want to admit that it was. I wanted the airplane part. I liked the idea of headsets, runways, and seeing the coast from above. I wasn’t sure how I felt about studying weather reports or learning what every little bolt was called. Those seemed like activities invented to keep people on the ground."},{"p":"Mom came in carrying grocery bags. She set them on the counter, dug out a carton of eggs, and looked at my paper."},{"p":"“Still fighting it?”"},{"p":"“I’m winning.”"},{"p":"“You haven’t written anything.”"},{"p":"“It’s a defensive strategy.”"},{"p":"She opened the refrigerator. “Try telling them what happened at baseball.”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Why not?”"},{"p":"“Because I looked stupid.”"},{"p":"“You were distracted. That’s different.”"},{"p":"“It didn’t look different to Coach.”"},{"p":"Mom closed the refrigerator with her hip. “You wanted to know where the airplane was going badly enough that you forgot where you were. Maybe start there.”"},{"p":"I looked at the sheet. Dad got up to help put away groceries. Neither of them asked if I was writing."},{"p":"That helped, irritatingly."},{"p":"I crossed out my first sentence and tried another."},{"p":"*I used to think pilots were people who already knew everything about airplanes.*"},{"p":"I stopped. That wasn’t quite right. I hadn’t thought about what pilots knew at all. I’d thought about what they got to do."},{"p":"I scratched a line through it so hard the pencil tore the paper."},{"p":"“Can I have another form?” I asked."},{"p":"Dad turned from the pantry. “We’ve got a printer.”"},{"p":"Mom made a noise."},{"p":"“Which,” he added, “we should approach with appropriate caution.”"},{"brk":1},{"p":"On Saturday, Everett let me into his garage through the side door. A fan hummed on a workbench. There were tomato plants outside the window and an old radio with its back removed beside a tray of screws."},{"p":"“Don’t touch that,” he said, before I touched anything."},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to.”"},{"p":"“You were looking at it with your hands.”"},{"p":"He cleared a spot on the bench and unrolled a faded aeronautical chart. It made a dry crackling sound, like paper that had spent years being folded the wrong way. Across it ran lines and circles and numbers that didn’t look remotely like a map anyone would use to get somewhere."},{"p":"“Where’s our house?” I asked."},{"p":"“Not marked.”"},{"p":"“Helpful.”"},{"p":"“Where’s the airport?”"},{"p":"I found Chesapeake, then searched around it. There were too many symbols. I pointed at one."},{"p":"“That’s not it,” Everett said."},{"p":"“Then why’d you ask?”"},{"p":"“To see where you’d start looking.”"},{"p":"I slid my finger across the chart. He didn’t move it for me. After a while I found the airport identifier printed beside the runways."},{"p":"“KPVG.”"},{"p":"“There you go.”"},{"p":"I expected him to tell me what every symbol meant. Instead, he handed me a pencil and asked me to find the coastline."},{"p":"“That’s easy.”"},{"p":"“Then find north.”"},{"p":"“Also easy.”"},{"p":"“Good. Two things you can do before lunch.”"},{"p":"He brought out a battered training manual and opened it to a drawing of an airplane surrounded by arrows. Lift, weight, thrust, drag. I’d seen the same picture online."},{"p":"“Four forces,” I said."},{"p":"“Mm-hmm.”"},{"p":"“I know those.”"},{"p":"“Explain drag.”"},{"p":"“Air pushing backward.”"},{"p":"“Sometimes that’s a useful way to picture it. What happens to drag when you change the airplane’s speed or configuration?”"},{"p":"I looked at the drawing. The arrows had no answers printed beside them."},{"p":"“Depends?”"},{"p":"“On what?”"},{"p":"I could feel myself getting annoyed. I hadn’t come over to take a test. Everett watched me with his crooked nose tilted slightly to one side, the way he did when an old radio made a noise he couldn’t identify."},{"p":"“I don’t know,” I said."},{"p":"“Good. Find out.”"},{"p":"He went back to his radio."},{"p":"For ten minutes I sat on a stool reading about drag while he sorted screws into little compartments. The manual had a diagram of a wing and more words than seemed necessary. Twice I caught myself rereading the same paragraph without understanding it. I almost asked Everett to explain, but he was using a soldering iron, and I’d been told not to interrupt somebody holding one."},{"p":"Eventually he unplugged it."},{"p":"“What’s your question?” he asked."},{"p":"“Why does adding flaps make more drag if the airplane needs lift?”"},{"p":"He looked pleased. Not because I knew the answer. Because I’d finally asked something specific."},{"p":"“Now,” he said, “we’ve got something to talk about.”"},{"brk":1},{"p":"That night I wrote my scholarship answer on a fresh sheet."},{"p":"I didn’t mention becoming an airline captain. I didn’t say I’d loved airplanes since I could walk, because I hadn’t. I wrote about missing the baseball and seeing the airplane. I wrote about how much I hated not knowing the difference between a flap and an aileron while Beckett did. I wrote that I wanted to find out whether I could learn something difficult without pretending it was easy."},{"p":"Reading it aloud made my face hot."},{"p":"Dad listened from the other end of the couch. He had a library book open but hadn’t turned the page."},{"p":"“Well?” I asked."},{"p":"“Sounds like you.”"},{"p":"“Is that good?”"},{"p":"“Mostly.”"},{"p":"I threw a cushion at him. He caught it with the book and told me not to bend the cover."}]},{"t":"Chapter Six — Before the Engine","blocks":[{"p":"On the morning of my first scheduled flight lesson, I expected an airplane with its door open."},{"p":"There was a clipboard."},{"p":"Mara handed it to me in the flight school’s small office. The air conditioner rattled above a shelf of headsets. Somebody had left half a powdered doughnut on a napkin beside the computer, and a printer kept spitting out pages with a noise like a trapped insect."},{"p":"“Morning, Lincoln. Before we go anywhere, we need to talk through today’s plan.”"},{"p":"I looked past her through the window. A white Cessna sat on the ramp. I recognized the high wing and the strut beneath it. The sight of it made my hands feel too big."},{"p":"“That’s ours?”"},{"p":"“It’s the aircraft scheduled for us, yes. Assuming everything checks out.”"},{"p":"“Looks fine from here.”"},{"p":"Mara followed my gaze. “You can tell that from a window?”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"She pointed to a chair. “Sit. We’ll review the weather, the lesson, and what you’re allowed to do. Then we’ll go outside together.”"},{"p":"Mom sat by the door with a paperback. She’d signed the paperwork and asked Mara enough questions that I was beginning to wonder if *she* wanted to learn to fly. Now she was pretending to read, though I could tell she was listening."},{"p":"Mara spread out a printed weather briefing. I could make out the airport identifier and a time. The rest looked like somebody had forgotten that spaces existed."},{"p":"“What’s this?” she asked."},{"p":"“A weather report.”"},{"p":"“Good. Which airport?”"},{"p":"I found KPVG and tapped it."},{"p":"“What can you tell me about the wind?”"},{"p":"I found the number but couldn’t remember how to read it. The clock on the wall clicked. I was aware of Mom turning a page she hadn’t finished."},{"p":"“I’m not sure.”"},{"p":"Mara turned the paper slightly. “That’s all right. We’re going to learn to read these. Today I want you to know why we check. Memorizing the codes can come later.”"},{"p":"She showed me where the wind information was, what the numbers represented, and why conditions on the ground mattered before anyone started an engine. She also explained that she’d make the operational decisions. I could help observe and ask questions, but she was the pilot in command."},{"p":"“Any questions?”"},{"p":"“How long until we fly?”"},{"p":"She glanced at the clock. “Depends on how the rest goes.”"},{"p":"I was starting to dislike that word."},{"p":"Outside, the air was warm enough that my shirt stuck between my shoulders before we’d reached the airplane. A fuel truck crawled along the far side of the ramp. I heard a starter turn, then the rough, rising sound of an engine catching."},{"p":"Mara stopped short of the wing."},{"p":"“First rule,” she said. “Stay with me. Don’t touch a switch or control unless I say so. And never go near the propeller— even when it’s stopped.”"},{"p":"I nodded."},{"p":"“Tell me back.”"},{"p":"I repeated it. She made me include the part about the propeller."},{"p":"The airplane looked different close up. Its paint was clean in some places and scuffed near the step. A faint dark streak ran back from a panel seam. The tire had little stones wedged in the grooves. I hadn’t expected it to look so used."},{"p":"Mara opened a checklist and began walking me through the preflight inspection. I held the paper while she pointed out the surfaces and explained what she was checking. She moved with the practiced patience of somebody who’d done this a thousand times and still expected to find something new."},{"p":"At the right wing, she paused."},{"p":"“Come look.”"},{"p":"A small metal cover near an inspection point wasn’t sitting flush. It was barely noticeable, the kind of thing I’d have stepped past without looking twice."},{"p":"“Is that bad?” I asked."},{"p":"“It’s something to check.”"},{"p":"“Could we just push it in?”"},{"p":"“Not until we know why it’s like that.”"},{"p":"She crouched and looked more closely without forcing it. Then she took out her phone and called someone from the maintenance side of the operation."},{"p":"I stood with the checklist flapping in the breeze."},{"p":"“So are we done?” I asked."},{"p":"“We’re waiting for an assessment.”"},{"p":"“It’s just a little cover.”"},{"p":"Mara straightened. “Maybe. But we don’t decide it’s safe by calling it little.”"},{"p":"I looked toward the runway. An airplane was taxiing out, its propeller a blurred disk in the sun. I could have been in ours by now. At least, that’s what I told myself."},{"p":"A mechanic came over carrying a small tool pouch. He greeted Mara, examined the cover, and spoke with her quietly. I caught words like *fastener* and *inspection* but couldn’t follow all of it. Nobody asked me to decide anything, which felt unfair until I realized I didn’t have the slightest idea what decision I’d make."},{"p":"The mechanic said he needed to check the maintenance records and the applicable instructions before anyone could decide whether the airplane was ready to fly. Mara thanked him."},{"p":"“How long?” I asked."},{"p":"“Not sure yet.”"},{"p":"“Can we use another plane?”"},{"p":"“Possibly, if one is available and suitable for today’s lesson. We won’t rush into that.”"},{"p":"She led me back toward the office. My shoes squeaked on the polished floor when we came inside. Mom looked up from her book."},{"p":"“Everything okay?”"},{"p":"“Maintenance is checking something,” Mara said. “No emergency. We’re pausing the flight until we know more.”"},{"p":"Mom nodded. I dropped into the chair beside her and pulled at the Velcro on my sneaker."},{"p":"“I thought we’d actually fly today.”"},{"p":"“So did I,” Mom said."},{"p":"Mara set the checklist on the table. “We still have time for a ground lesson if you’d like. Or we can reschedule the entire session. Either way, you won’t be charged for flight time we don’t fly.”"},{"p":"I didn’t answer. Through the window, the Cessna sat in exactly the same place, sun shining off its windshield."},{"p":"Mom put a finger between the pages of her book. “It’s your call about the ground lesson.”"},{"p":"I hated that she wasn’t telling me what to do."},{"p":"“What would we do?” I asked Mara."},{"p":"“We could sit in a secured airplane if the staff approves it. Otherwise, we’ll use the training room and learn the panel.”"},{"p":"“More checklists.”"},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"I pressed my thumb into the seam of the chair. Mara didn’t try to make the lesson sound exciting. That was the irritating thing about her. She never seemed worried that I’d lose interest."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said. “The ground lesson.”"},{"p":"She picked up the clipboard. “All right. First, let’s find out which room is free.”"},{"p":"Mom went back to her book. I followed Mara down the hallway, still listening for the sound of the engine we hadn’t started."}]},{"t":"Chapter Seven — The Empty Seat","blocks":[{"p":"The training room had a plastic model airplane hanging from the ceiling by fishing line. Somebody had tied it badly. Its left wing hung lower than the right, and the nose pointed toward a water stain in the corner."},{"p":"I liked it immediately. It was the first thing in the building that seemed to be having a worse morning than I was."},{"p":"Mara rolled a chair over to the table and spread out a laminated picture of the Skyhawk’s instrument panel. She set a small, heavy book beside it. The book had bent corners and several little flags sticking out of the pages."},{"p":"“Can I ask something before we start?” I said."},{"p":"“Sure.”"},{"p":"“Did you know that cover was loose when we got here?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Then why didn’t you seem surprised?”"},{"p":"She pulled a pencil from behind her ear. “I was surprised. I just didn’t need to make it your problem.”"},{"p":"“It was my problem. I was supposed to fly.”"},{"p":"“You were supposed to learn.”"},{"p":"“That sounds like something adults say when a fun thing gets canceled.”"},{"p":"Mara looked at me over the top of the panel picture. “Sometimes it is.”"},{"p":"I waited for the rest of the lecture. She uncapped a dry-erase marker instead."},{"p":"“Find the airspeed indicator.”"},{"p":"I knew that one. I’d watched a video about the six basic flight instruments twice, once at regular speed and once at a speed that made the narrator sound like he’d swallowed a battery."},{"p":"I tapped the dial in the upper left."},{"p":"“Good. What does it tell you?”"},{"p":"“How fast you’re going.”"},{"p":"“Through what?”"},{"p":"I hesitated. “The sky?”"},{"p":"“Through the air,” she said, drawing an arrow. “The instrument uses pressure measurements to show indicated airspeed. Now imagine flying into a headwind.”"},{"p":"“I know what a headwind is.”"},{"p":"“Then explain what happens to your groundspeed.”"},{"p":"My mouth opened, but I had to picture it first: an airplane trying to move forward while wind pushed against it. “The airplane could be moving over the ground more slowly than it’s moving through the air.”"},{"p":"“Right. Groundspeed is how fast you move over the ground. Indicated airspeed and true airspeed are different, too—but one problem at a time.”"},{"p":"I looked at the instrument again. The needle was painted at zero, but the face had colored arcs and numbers that went much higher than my bicycle speedometer."},{"p":"She had me find the altimeter next, then the attitude indicator. When I confused the heading indicator with the turn coordinator, she didn’t say anything until I caught myself."},{"p":"“Wait. No. This one.”"},{"p":"“What changed your mind?”"},{"p":"“The little airplane looks different.”"},{"p":"“That’s a start. Tell me what each display is actually showing.”"},{"p":"I leaned closer. The marker smelled sharp enough to make my eyes water. I knew the instrument names. What they actually told a pilot was another problem."},{"p":"Twenty minutes later, she took out a little foam airplane. Its left wing had a tooth mark in it."},{"p":"“Did somebody bite that?” I asked."},{"p":"“A student. Several years ago.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“He was six. I didn’t ask.”"},{"p":"She held the airplane level between us. “If I raise the nose like this, what do you think the airplane will do?”"},{"p":"“Climb.”"},{"p":"“Always?”"},{"p":"I studied her face. She wasn’t smiling, which meant the answer was probably no."},{"p":"“I don’t know.”"},{"p":"“Good answer.”"},{"p":"“It’s not an answer.”"},{"p":"“It is when you don’t know. Raising the nose changes the airplane’s attitude. Whether it climbs depends on what else is happening—including power and airspeed.” She lowered the model. “We’ll work through those relationships over time.”"},{"p":"Every answer about flying seemed to come with three more questions. The airplane in the picture had wings, a tail, and an engine. How complicated could it be? Apparently, very."},{"p":"There was a knock at the door. A man in dark work trousers leaned in and spoke quietly to Mara. I heard the words *inspection* and *paperwork*. Mara thanked him. The man left."},{"p":"“Are we flying?” I asked."},{"p":"“Not in that aircraft today.”"},{"p":"I pressed my lips together."},{"p":"“Maintenance found a fastener that needed attention,” she said. “They’re following up on it. It might be a simple fix, but we don’t fly until the issue is properly resolved.”"},{"p":"“Could we go look at another one?”"},{"p":"“We can see whether the other Skyhawk is available for a static cockpit visit. No flight. We already made today’s plan.”"},{"p":"She stood and waited while I put the marker caps back on. One had rolled under the table. I crouched to get it and bumped my head coming up."},{"p":"“Careful,” Mara said."},{"p":"“Too late.”"},{"p":"Outside, the heat had settled over the ramp. We followed the marked pedestrian path with Mara ahead of me. The second Skyhawk was parked near a hangar, chocked and tied down. Mara confirmed with the operations desk that it was available for a supervised visit, then checked that the aircraft was secured before she opened the door."},{"p":"The cabin smelled like warm plastic, old fabric, and something sharp that reminded me of a tool drawer. I put one sneaker on the step, ducked beneath the wing, and climbed into the left seat."},{"p":"For a second I forgot to breathe."},{"p":"The windshield wrapped around the front of me. I could see the taxiway and the pale strip of runway beyond it. A little bug crawled across the outside of the glass. The seats were narrower than I expected. My knees didn’t quite know where to go."},{"p":"“Keep your feet clear of the pedals for now,” Mara said from the open right door. “Hands in your lap.”"},{"p":"I folded them so tightly my knuckles turned white."},{"p":"She pointed out the yoke, rudder pedals, throttle, mixture control, and the panel we’d been studying. Nothing was lit. The airplane was quiet enough that I could hear a small metallic tapping from the tiedown rope against the wing strut."},{"p":"“Can I hold the yoke?”"},{"p":"“In a moment. First, tell me what moves when you turn it.”"},{"p":"“The ailerons.”"},{"p":"“And when you push or pull?”"},{"p":"“The elevator.”"},{"p":"She nodded and, after making sure the controls were clear, let me move the yoke gently while she watched. The motion was smaller than I’d imagined. No heroic sweeping gestures. Just a few inches of travel and a quiet resistance under my hands."},{"p":"I wanted to pull it all the way back and pretend the nose was lifting into the sky. Instead, I stopped where she told me."},{"p":"“Good,” she said. “Now center it.”"},{"p":"I did."},{"p":"A truck passed behind us. Mara waited for it to clear before speaking again."},{"p":"“That’s enough for today.”"},{"p":"“Already?”"},{"p":"“You’ve been here nearly two hours.”"},{"p":"I looked at the clock on the panel. It wasn’t running. I had no idea what time it was."},{"p":"Mara stepped back so I could climb out. My shirt snagged on the seat belt buckle, and I had to turn sideways to free it. By the time my feet touched the pavement, the cabin already looked like somebody else’s place."},{"p":"At the office, Mom asked whether I’d had fun."},{"p":"“Not really,” I said."},{"p":"Mara handed her the lesson notes. “He did good work.”"},{"p":"Mom glanced at me. “Sounds like you did.”"},{"p":"I didn’t correct either of them. In the car, I held the folded instrument diagram against my knee and traced the circle of the attitude indicator with my thumbnail until the paper went soft."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eight — Beckett’s Bad Timing","blocks":[{"p":"The next academy meeting was on Thursday afternoon. Dad dropped me off ten minutes early because he had to get back for an evening program at the library, and the lobby smelled like microwave popcorn."},{"p":"Beckett sat on the floor beside a vending machine, trying to pull a bent zipper around an overstuffed backpack."},{"p":"“It’s caught,” I said."},{"p":"“I can see that.”"},{"p":"“Maybe take some stuff out.”"},{"p":"He looked up at me through his square glasses. “Brilliant. You should work in maintenance.”"},{"p":"I sat on the bench and watched him fight with it. A corner of a thick notebook stuck out of the opening, along with a little plastic propeller and what looked like three screwdrivers."},{"p":"“Why do you have tools in your school bag?”"},{"p":"“Because I need them.”"},{"p":"“For school?”"},{"p":"“For things.”"},{"p":"He tugged the zipper. It came free so fast he nearly hit himself in the face."},{"p":"“See?” he said."},{"p":"“Great technique.”"},{"p":"He shoved the notebook inside. “Heard your flight got canceled.”"},{"p":"“It wasn’t canceled. We did a ground lesson.”"},{"p":"“So you didn’t fly.”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Then it got canceled.”"},{"p":"I watched a woman at the reception counter sort a stack of forms. “It was a maintenance thing.”"},{"p":"“Which airplane?”"},{"p":"I told him."},{"p":"“Was it the inspection cover on the wing?”"},{"p":"I turned. “How’d you know?”"},{"p":"“My uncle works on airplanes. He was talking to somebody about a fastener they were checking.”"},{"p":"“Did he say it was nothing?”"},{"p":"“He said he hadn’t seen it.”"},{"p":"“Well, it looked like nothing.”"},{"p":"Beckett stopped messing with his bag. “You didn’t even know what it was.”"},{"p":"“I knew it was a cover.”"},{"p":"“That’s not the same.”"},{"p":"“Why are you acting like I broke it?”"},{"p":"“I’m not.”"},{"p":"“You keep talking about it.”"},{"p":"“Because you asked.”"},{"p":"I hadn’t, exactly. But I was tired of people telling me what I didn’t know, and Beckett had an especially efficient way of doing it."},{"p":"Rafe came through the front door with a paper bag in one hand. “Anybody want pretzels?”"},{"p":"“Sure,” Beckett said."},{"p":"I shook my head."},{"p":"Rafe looked between us. “Did I miss something?”"},{"p":"“No,” I said."},{"p":"“Okay.” He sat down and ate a pretzel. “Feels like something.”"},{"p":"Imani arrived a minute later carrying a notebook with little weather symbols drawn along the edge. She sat beside Rafe, opened to a clean page, and asked if anyone had done the reading."},{"p":"Beckett said yes. Rafe said mostly. I said I’d looked at it, which was true in the strictest possible sense."},{"p":"Our assignment had been to read two pages about the four forces of flight and come prepared to explain one of them without using the book. I’d planned to do it after dinner. Then I’d watched a video about the cockpit. Then another one about landings. By the time I remembered the assignment, Mom was telling me to brush my teeth."},{"p":"In the classroom, the academy coordinator divided us into pairs. Naturally, I got Beckett."},{"p":"We were supposed to build a paper glider and test one change at a time. The instructions fit on a single sheet. Beckett read them first, flattening the page with his palm."},{"p":"“I’ve made these before,” I said."},{"p":"“Me too.”"},{"p":"“Then we don’t need instructions.”"},{"p":"“We need to know what we’re measuring.”"},{"p":"“We’re throwing paper airplanes.”"},{"p":"“Right. And measuring what happens.”"},{"p":"He handed me the paper. I folded the nose sharply, pinched the center crease, and bent the wings into what I thought was a perfect shape. Beckett started to say something, but I was already finished."},{"p":"“Watch,” I said."},{"p":"I launched it across the room."},{"p":"It flew beautifully for about three feet, rolled left, and struck the leg of a chair."},{"p":"Rafe made a sympathetic noise. “That looked expensive.”"},{"p":"Beckett picked up the glider. He didn’t laugh, which somehow made it worse. He held it at eye level and sighted along the wings."},{"p":"“Your left wing’s lower,” he said."},{"p":"“Barely.”"},{"p":"“It’s enough to try fixing.”"},{"p":"He adjusted the fold and gave it back. I threw it again. This time it went farther before turning into a bookshelf."},{"p":"“Still bad,” I said."},{"p":"“Less bad.”"},{"p":"“That should be on the academy brochure.”"},{"p":"Imani looked up from the other table. “Are you changing more than one thing each time?”"},{"p":"“No,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Yes,” I said at the same time."},{"p":"She raised her eyebrows."},{"p":"Beckett spread the glider on the desk. “We should make another one exactly the same, then change one part. Otherwise we won’t know what helped.”"},{"p":"“We only have twenty minutes.”"},{"p":"“Then stop arguing.”"},{"p":"I stared at him. He stared back."},{"p":"The coordinator walked past, checking the groups. I could have complained that Beckett was bossy. Instead, I took a fresh sheet and folded it more carefully. The paper made a crisp sound beneath my thumbnail. Beckett held the corners together while I pressed the center crease."},{"p":"We made three gliders. One had wider wings. One had a little more weight at the nose from a paper clip the coordinator supplied. The third was our original design, as close as we could make it."},{"p":"We tested them in the hallway, launching from a strip of tape on the floor. Imani recorded distances for her group, then offered to keep ours too. Rafe stood near the far wall and called out where each glider landed, loudly enough that the coordinator told him to use his indoor voice."},{"p":"The wide-wing glider floated farther than I expected. The one with the paper clip near its nose dived almost immediately. Our original wobbled but went straight."},{"p":"Beckett wrote the results in neat columns. His handwriting was so small I had to lean over to read it."},{"p":"“Why’d the heavy one dive?” I asked."},{"p":"“Maybe the balance changed too much.”"},{"p":"“Maybe?”"},{"p":"“We’d have to test it again. And check the folds.”"},{"p":"I nudged the paper clip with my finger. “So you don’t know.”"},{"p":"“Nope.” He looked pleased about it."},{"p":"That annoyed me more than it should have."},{"p":"After class, Mara came into the lobby to speak with a parent. She saw me and asked whether I’d been reviewing the instrument panel."},{"p":"“A little,” I said."},{"p":"“Bring your notes next time. We’ll build on them.”"},{"p":"“Are we going to fly?”"},{"p":"“If the weather’s good and we have an available instructor and airplane, yes.”"},{"p":"“That is the longest way anybody’s ever said maybe.”"},{"p":"She smiled, but only briefly. “You can tell me what you think of the wording after you finish your reading.”"},{"p":"Beckett made a choking noise beside me."},{"p":"I pretended not to hear it."},{"p":"Outside, Dad’s car hadn’t arrived yet. The late sunlight lay across the parking lot in long yellow bars. Beckett sat on the curb with his bag between his feet. I stood a few steps away, watching a small airplane pass over the hangars."},{"p":"“Hey,” he said."},{"p":"I turned."},{"p":"“You want the measurements?” He held out his notebook. “From the gliders.”"},{"p":"“I have them.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"He closed the book."},{"p":"I looked at the airplane until it disappeared behind a building. Then I walked back and sat on the curb, leaving a foot of concrete between us."},{"p":"“Actually,” I said, “I missed the last two.”"},{"p":"Beckett opened the notebook again. “Your handwriting is probably better than mine.”"},{"p":"“Not a chance.”"},{"p":"He tilted the page toward me, and I copied the numbers while the sun warmed the back of my neck."}]},{"t":"Chapter Nine — The Right Seat","blocks":[{"p":"The first thing I noticed about Mara’s airplane was the smell."},{"p":"Not gasoline, exactly. Warm plastic, old upholstery, a trace of something oily underneath. The cabin had spent the morning in the sun, and when Mara opened the door a pocket of hot air came out against my face. A thin white scratch ran along the lower edge of the doorframe. I touched it before I could stop myself."},{"p":"“Bag in the back,” Mara said. “Headset stays with you.”"},{"p":"I climbed into the left seat, then remembered the bag was still on my shoulder. Getting it off without hitting the yoke required a complicated arrangement of elbows. Mara waited outside, one hand resting on the door, while I fought the strap."},{"p":"“Would it be easier,” she asked, “if you got out first?”"},{"p":"“Probably.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"I got out. Put the bag behind the seats. Got back in. It was a humiliating amount of work for something I’d imagined doing in one smooth motion."},{"p":"Mom stood beyond the marked area with her arms folded against the wind. She raised a hand. I waved, then wished I hadn’t, because I nearly caught the headset cord on the seat belt."},{"p":"Mara climbed into the right seat. She’d already completed the exterior inspection with me, taking her time at every point, even the parts I was sure we’d covered twice. The airplane had been returned to service after the fastener issue that had kept us on the ground last time. She’d checked the maintenance status before we went outside. I’d asked her twice whether she was certain."},{"p":"She hadn’t minded the first question. The second got a look."},{"p":"“Do you want me to be certain,” she’d said, “or do you want me to tell you yes quickly?”"},{"p":"I hadn’t had a good answer."},{"p":"Now she showed me how the lap belt and shoulder harness fitted together. I tightened mine until it pressed into my hip."},{"p":"“Not quite that tight. You need to breathe.”"},{"p":"“I can breathe.”"},{"p":"“Try a full breath.”"},{"p":"I loosened it."},{"p":"Outside, a fuel truck crept past the hangar. Its amber light flashed without making a sound. Somewhere behind us a propeller began turning, slow enough at first that I could follow the blades, then fast enough to disappear."},{"p":"Mara handed me a laminated checklist. “You read. I’ll do the items. If I ask you to touch anything, I’ll be specific.”"},{"p":"I’d practiced the words at home. In the cockpit, they seemed to have rearranged themselves."},{"p":"“Seats and belts,” I read."},{"p":"She checked them. We moved down the list. The airplane stayed quiet while she worked, then the engine started with a shudder that traveled through the soles of my shoes. The headset pressed my hair flat. Through it, Mara’s voice sounded both closer and farther away."},{"p":"“Can you hear me?”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Try again without shouting.”"},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“Better.”"},{"p":"I looked at the panel. The needles I’d studied on paper were alive now. One trembled. Another sat steady. The engine’s vibration made the edge of the checklist buzz against my knee."},{"p":"Mara handled the radio. She explained what she was doing, but didn’t pretend I could learn all of it in one sitting. We taxied at walking speed, following the painted centerline. A row of hangars slid past my window."},{"p":"The airplane felt larger on the ground than it had when I’d sat in it with the engine off. The wings were out there somewhere beyond my shoulders, wider than the taxiway looked. I kept checking that we weren’t going to clip anything."},{"p":"“Are you steering with the yoke?” I asked."},{"p":"“Not with the yoke. The rudder pedals control the steerable nosewheel on this Skyhawk. The yoke moves the ailerons and elevator.”"},{"p":"I looked down at my feet, carefully clear of the pedals."},{"p":"“Could I try?”"},{"p":"“Not during this part.”"},{"p":"“Right.”"},{"p":"She glanced over. “You’re going to hear that word a lot.”"},{"p":"“I noticed.”"},{"p":"At the run-up area she stopped and worked through another checklist. The engine changed pitch while she checked it. I watched a little airplane land in the distance, its wheels making contact so gently I couldn’t tell the exact instant it touched down."},{"p":"I had pictured our takeoff at least a hundred times. Every version involved me staring straight ahead, brave and calm, while the airplane climbed into a perfect blue sky."},{"p":"In the real version, I was worried about the belt rubbing my neck."},{"p":"Mara briefed me on what she’d do if anything went wrong before or after takeoff. She spoke plainly, with no dramatic pauses. I listened harder than I had to anything in weeks."},{"p":"After checking for traffic, including on final approach, Mara announced our departure intentions over the CTAF and taxied onto the runway."},{"p":"The centerline stripes went straight ahead until they narrowed into white dashes near the trees. Mara aligned the airplane. I had enough time to wonder whether Mom could still see us."},{"p":"The engine grew louder."},{"p":"We rolled."},{"p":"The seat pressed against my back. The runway markings began coming faster, and the airplane made little corrections beneath us, not smooth like a video game but busy, alive, demanding attention. I kept my hands in my lap. My fingers were locked together so tightly that one thumbnail left a half-moon in the skin of the other."},{"p":"Then the rattling changed. The wheels were no longer on the ground."},{"p":"I didn’t cheer. I didn’t say anything at all."},{"p":"A road passed beneath the left wing, followed by a patchwork of trees and roofs. The horizon looked impossibly far away. I had thought the first thing I’d feel would be freedom. Instead, I felt the uneasy knowledge that the floor of the airplane was thin and the ground was not where floors were supposed to be."},{"p":"“You doing okay?” Mara asked."},{"p":"I nodded, then remembered she was looking ahead."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Look outside. Pick a point on the horizon.”"},{"p":"I did. My breathing slowed enough that I noticed the sun on my left forearm."},{"p":"Once we were at a safe altitude in the practice area and she’d completed the appropriate checks, Mara demonstrated gentle changes in pitch and bank. She talked about the horizon, not the instrument needles. She showed me how little movement was needed."},{"p":"“Would you like to try?”"},{"p":"The answer had been yes for weeks. I still had to swallow before saying it."},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"She said, “You have the flight controls,” and waited for my reply before letting go."},{"p":"“I have the flight controls.”"},{"p":"The yoke felt different with the airplane moving through the air. I held it with both hands, afraid that relaxing one finger would send us rolling toward the ocean."},{"p":"“Try keeping the nose where it is,” Mara said."},{"p":"I looked at the cowling, then the horizon. The nose rose a little. I pushed forward. It dipped too far. I pulled back. The airplane climbed again."},{"p":"“Smaller corrections.”"},{"p":"“I am making small corrections.”"},{"p":"“Smaller than those.”"},{"p":"My cheeks grew hot. I tried to hold perfectly still, which didn’t work either. A bump lifted us. I stiffened and pulled back without meaning to."},{"p":"Mara’s voice stayed level. “I have the flight controls,” she said."},{"p":"“You have the flight controls,” I answered."},{"p":"“I have the flight controls,” she confirmed."},{"p":"Her hands settled on the yoke. The nose steadied."},{"p":"I stared at the instrument panel."},{"p":"“You were chasing the nose,” she said. “You saw it move and corrected too much. That’s common at first.”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t scared.”"},{"p":"“I didn’t ask if you were.”"},{"p":"“I just thought—”"},{"p":"She waited."},{"p":"“I thought I’d be better at it.”"},{"p":"Mara looked out through the windshield. “Why?”"},{"p":"Because I’d watched videos. Because I’d read the brochure twelve times. Because I’d imagined this so often that the real thing was supposed to recognize me."},{"p":"“I don’t know,” I said."},{"p":"She didn’t argue. A few minutes later, after another demonstration, she let me try again. This time I held the yoke more loosely. The nose still wandered. Not as much."},{"p":"On the way back she flew the approach and landing. I watched her hands, then the runway, then her hands again. The wheels touched with a bump I felt in my teeth."},{"p":"“That’s normal,” she said, though I hadn’t spoken."},{"p":"Back at the parking spot, the engine stopped and the silence rushed into the cabin. I could hear a metal tick as something cooled beneath the cowling."},{"p":"Mara removed her headset. “What surprised you?”"},{"p":"I unbuckled, but the shoulder strap caught under my arm."},{"p":"“Everything.”"},{"p":"“Pick one thing.”"},{"p":"I freed the strap. “How much I moved the controls. I thought I’d barely touch them.”"},{"p":"“You did barely need to touch them.”"},{"p":"I looked at her."},{"p":"“That’s the part we’re going to practice.”"},{"p":"Mom was waiting beyond the gate. I could see her through the windshield, one hand shading her eyes. I wanted to tell her I’d flown an airplane. That was true, technically. I also wanted to tell her I’d been terrible at it, which felt true in a much larger way."},{"p":"Mara opened her door. “Bring your notebook next time. Write down what the airplane did, not what you wished it had done.”"},{"p":"I stayed seated long enough for her to look back at me."},{"p":"“Lincoln?”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“You can get out now.”"}]},{"t":"Chapter Ten — The Part I Left Out","blocks":[{"p":"At dinner, Dad asked what the clouds looked like from above."},{"p":"I was cutting a piece of chicken into pieces so small they were barely worth eating. “There weren’t many.”"},{"p":"“Any turbulence?”"},{"p":"“A little.”"},{"p":"“Did you get to use the controls?”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"Mom set down her fork. “You did?”"},{"p":"“For a little while.”"},{"p":"“That’s wonderful.”"},{"p":"I shrugged and reached for the water pitcher."},{"p":"Dad asked what it felt like. I told him the yoke was heavier than I’d expected. That was the part I could describe without sounding foolish. I didn’t mention how my hands had tightened or how quickly Mara had taken control back."},{"p":"“You must be thrilled,” Mom said."},{"p":"“Sure.”"},{"p":"She watched me pour water. I spilled some beside the glass and wiped it with my sleeve before she could say anything."},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“What?”"},{"p":"“You don’t sound thrilled.”"},{"p":"“I am.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"She picked up her fork again, but the okay hung there, not entirely convinced."},{"p":"Dad changed the subject to a patron at the library who had returned a book with a pancake pressed between the pages. I laughed at the right places. The story was probably exaggerated; Dad had a habit of improving things in the telling, especially when the truth involved someone being careless with a book."},{"p":"After dinner I carried my plate to the sink and went upstairs. My headset sat on the bed where I’d dropped it. One ear cushion had twisted sideways. I straightened it, then set it on the desk."},{"p":"The notebook lay underneath."},{"p":"I opened to a clean page."},{"p":"WHAT THE AIRPLANE DID, I wrote."},{"p":"Then nothing."},{"p":"Outside my window, a neighbor’s dog barked twice. A car door slammed. I drew a line under the heading, pressing hard enough that the pencil point broke."},{"p":"I sharpened it over the trash can and tried again."},{"p":"The nose went up and down because I moved the yoke too much."},{"p":"I crossed out because."},{"p":"The nose went up and down. I moved the yoke too much."},{"p":"Better. Less like an excuse."},{"p":"I wrote down the sequence: nose high, push, nose low, pull. I added the part about tightening my grip when we hit a bump. The words looked childish, like notes from someone who’d never been near an airplane. Which, until that afternoon, had been true."},{"p":"My phone buzzed."},{"p":"Rafe: DID YOU FLY OR DID THEY MAKE YOU COUNT BOLTS AGAIN"},{"p":"I typed: Flew."},{"p":"Three dots appeared immediately."},{"p":"Rafe: DETAILS"},{"p":"I stared at the message. It would have been easy to say it was amazing. He’d send a row of airplane emojis, and tomorrow at the academy I’d be the kid who’d finally flown."},{"p":"Instead I typed: I kept chasing the nose up and down."},{"p":"Rafe: so you crashed"},{"p":"Me: No."},{"p":"Rafe: good start then"},{"p":"I laughed, a short sound I hadn’t expected."},{"p":"Rafe: serious though was it cool"},{"p":"I thought about the road slipping beneath the wing. The hot headset cups. The way the engine noise had filled my whole body until the wheels lifted and the sound changed."},{"p":"Me: Yeah. Really cool."},{"p":"I put the phone down."},{"p":"A knock came at my door. Mom leaned in, still wearing the dish towel over one shoulder."},{"p":"“Can I come in?”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"She sat on the edge of the bed. One of the springs squeaked. Her eyes went to the notebook, but she didn’t reach for it."},{"p":"“Was it different from what you expected?”"},{"p":"I turned the pencil between my fingers."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Different good or different bad?”"},{"p":"“Both.”"},{"p":"She nodded, as if that made perfect sense."},{"p":"“I couldn’t keep it straight,” I said. “Mara showed me how, and then I did it wrong. Twice.”"},{"p":"Mom glanced at the headset. “Did she expect you to do it perfectly?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Did you?”"},{"p":"I rolled the pencil across the desk. It hit the lamp base and stopped."},{"p":"“I guess.”"},{"p":"She reached over and turned the notebook so she could read the first few lines. “Looks like you learned something.”"},{"p":"“I learned I’m not very good at flying.”"},{"p":"“You had one lesson.”"},{"p":"“That’s what everybody keeps saying.”"},{"p":"“Maybe because it’s true.”"},{"p":"I pulled the notebook back. “You don’t get it.”"},{"p":"She stood. For a second I thought she was going to leave without answering. Instead, she picked up the dish towel from her shoulder and folded it into a square."},{"p":"“Maybe I don’t,” she said. “I haven’t flown a plane. But I know what it’s like to work hard for something and still have a lousy first day.”"},{"p":"“It wasn’t lousy.”"},{"p":"“You just told me—”"},{"p":"“It wasn’t.”"},{"p":"Mom looked at me for a long second. “All right.”"},{"p":"She set the folded towel on my dresser and went to the door."},{"p":"“Mom?”"},{"p":"She stopped."},{"p":"“I did like it.”"},{"p":"“I believe you.”"},{"p":"After she left, I opened the notebook again. At the bottom of the page I wrote one more line."},{"p":"Next time: look outside more. Stop squeezing the yoke."},{"p":"I underlined the second sentence. The pencil tore a little hole in the paper."},{"brk":1},{"p":"At the next academy meeting, Imani had a weather chart spread across the table before anybody else arrived. She’d marked a line of wind arrows in the margin with a ruler."},{"p":"I sat across from her."},{"p":"“Did you really fly yesterday?” she asked."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“How was it?”"},{"p":"I took my notebook out of my bag. The torn page stuck to the one behind it."},{"p":"“Harder than I thought.”"},{"p":"She looked up. “Which part?”"},{"p":"I slid the notebook across the table."},{"p":"Imani read it, tapping one finger beside the line about the yoke. “That makes sense.”"},{"p":"“Does it?”"},{"p":"“My cousin tried steering a canoe last summer. He kept correcting so hard that we went from one bank to the other.”"},{"p":"“An airplane isn’t a canoe.”"},{"p":"“I know.” She pushed the notebook back. “But people are people.”"},{"p":"I wanted to argue with that. It was the kind of thing an adult would say, and Imani had said it without even looking pleased with herself."},{"p":"Instead, I opened to a fresh page."},{"p":"“What’s with all the arrows?” I asked."},{"p":"“Wind. We’re supposed to compare what it does at different heights.”"},{"p":"I leaned forward. Some arrows pointed one way, others another."},{"p":"“Why are they different?”"},{"p":"“That’s what I’m trying to figure out.”"},{"p":"She moved her ruler aside so I could see the numbers underneath."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eleven — The Cooler Between Us","blocks":[{"p":"Mom’s idea of packing for a weekend trip involved three lists, two laundry baskets, and a pencil tucked behind her ear. Dad’s involved putting a paperback in his back pocket and asking where the car keys were."},{"p":"Mine involved the weather."},{"p":"“Wind’s supposed to be off the ocean tomorrow,” I said from the kitchen doorway."},{"p":"Mom held up a plastic container. “Does that mean you can carry the sandwiches?”"},{"p":"“It’s supposed to pick up in the afternoon.”"},{"p":"“Interesting. Sandwiches?”"},{"p":"I took the container. One corner of the lid wasn’t snapped down. I fixed it before Mom could notice."},{"p":"Dad came through with the cooler, dragging one corner along the floor. “We can discuss the weather in the car. Right now we have a serious problem.”"},{"p":"“What?”"},{"p":"“Somebody has packed enough food to survive a winter.”"},{"p":"“We’re going for two days,” Mom said."},{"p":"“Exactly. An ambitious winter.”"},{"p":"He lifted the cooler onto the counter. The handle fell off."},{"p":"It hit the tile with a sharp clack."},{"p":"Nobody spoke."},{"p":"Dad picked it up and turned it over in his hands. “Well. That answers the question of whether we should have replaced this.”"},{"p":"Mom closed her eyes. “Adrian.”"},{"p":"“I was going to.”"},{"p":"“You said that in June.”"},{"p":"“It’s still June.”"},{"p":"“Last June.”"},{"p":"I slipped past them with the sandwiches. We’d been talking about the Outer Banks for weeks, ever since Mom found a little rental place that was cheaper than the hotels near the beach. Dad had promised we could stop at the Wright Brothers National Memorial on Saturday. Uncle Everett was driving down separately to meet us there."},{"p":"I had looked at pictures of the hill, the stone monument, the reconstructed buildings, and the long stretch of ground where the first powered flights had taken place. I knew the Wright brothers’ first powered flight lasted about twelve seconds. I’d timed twelve seconds on my phone three different ways, because it sounded like nothing at all."},{"p":"At school, twelve seconds was how long it took Rafe to convince a teacher he’d forgotten his homework in a locker he didn’t have."},{"p":"In an airplane, apparently, twelve seconds could change history."},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"Mom was holding out a roll of paper towels."},{"p":"“Sorry.”"},{"p":"“Trunk. Please.”"},{"p":"Outside, the air had that thick, wet feeling Virginia got before breakfast in summer. The car smelled faintly of old French fries and the pine-scented cleaner Dad used whenever Mom complained about the old French fries. I put the paper towels beside my backpack and checked that my notebook was still inside."},{"p":"Dad came out carrying the cooler by its bottom."},{"p":"“Don’t say anything,” he told me."},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to.”"},{"p":"“You had the face.”"},{"p":"“What face?”"},{"p":"“The one that says you’re about to say something.”"},{"p":"I helped him ease the cooler into the trunk. When he straightened, he pressed one hand into the small of his back and made the same noise he made getting out of a low chair."},{"p":"“Do you think,” I asked, “the Wright brothers had this many problems just getting to the beach?”"},{"p":"Dad looked at the cooler. “Probably more. They didn’t have a car.”"},{"p":"“That’s not really the same.”"},{"p":"“No,” he said. “They probably had a better cooler.”"},{"brk":1},{"p":"We made it past the state line before Mom realized she’d left the bag with the sunscreen on the kitchen counter."},{"p":"Dad didn’t turn around. He found a pharmacy on the way, and Mom bought sunscreen at a price that made her stare at the receipt for a full minute."},{"p":"“That’s more than lunch,” she said."},{"p":"“It’s a very small bottle of protection,” Dad offered."},{"p":"She handed him the receipt. He stopped talking."},{"p":"I was in the back seat with my notebook balanced on one knee. I had drawn a Cessna from memory. The left wing was shorter than the right, and the nose looked like it belonged to a duck."},{"p":"The first lesson kept returning to me at odd times: Mara’s hand resting near the controls without touching them, the horizon moving when I pushed too hard, my shoulders creeping toward my ears. She’d told me to use pressure, not muscle. I could remember her saying it. I couldn’t remember doing it."},{"p":"“Are you going to draw the whole trip?” Mom asked."},{"p":"“Maybe.”"},{"p":"“There’s a camera on your phone.”"},{"p":"“That’s different.”"},{"p":"She turned around. “How?”"},{"p":"I looked at my crooked airplane. “When I draw something, I notice what’s wrong with it.”"},{"p":"Dad made a small approving noise. I wished he hadn’t. It made my answer sound like something I’d practiced."},{"p":"Traffic slowed near a bridge. Through the windshield I could see red brake lights all the way to a bend in the road. Dad eased off the accelerator."},{"p":"“That’s not good,” he said."},{"p":"I looked at the clock. We were already behind the schedule I’d written in the margin of my notebook."},{"p":"“Can we take another road?”"},{"p":"“Not one that helps.”"},{"p":"“How long?”"},{"p":"“No idea.”"},{"p":"I checked the traffic map. A dark red line stretched ahead of us."},{"p":"“Forty-six minutes,” I said."},{"p":"Dad leaned toward the windshield as though he could see the end of the line. “Wonderful.”"},{"p":"“It’s going to mess up everything.”"},{"p":"Mom turned around again. “What everything?”"},{"p":"“The plan.”"},{"p":"“What plan?”"},{"p":"“The one I made.”"},{"p":"“Lincoln, we’re going to a rental house and then getting dinner. We don’t have tickets for anything tonight.”"},{"p":"“I know. But I wanted to be there before—”"},{"p":"“Before what?”"},{"p":"I didn’t have an answer that wouldn’t sound ridiculous. Before the time I’d written down. Before I had to cross something out."},{"p":"The cars in front of us crawled forward. Dad drummed his thumbs against the wheel, stopped when Mom looked at him, then started again on his knee."},{"p":"“Your uncle would have a field day with this,” Dad said."},{"p":"“With traffic?”"},{"p":"“With somebody getting upset because the world didn’t follow his plan.”"},{"p":"I stared out the window."},{"p":"A pickup in the next lane had a kayak tied to its roof with orange straps. Every time the traffic moved, the kayak shifted slightly. The driver didn’t seem to notice."},{"p":"“Those straps look loose,” I said."},{"p":"Dad glanced over. “They do.”"},{"p":"“Should we tell him?”"},{"p":"“We can if we end up beside him at a stop.”"},{"p":"For the next ten minutes, I watched the kayak instead of the clock."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twelve — Twelve Seconds","blocks":[{"p":"The wind grabbed the car door before I had both feet on the ground."},{"p":"I grabbed it with my left hand and nearly knocked my knee against the frame. Sand hissed across the parking lot in little bursts. Farther up the path, the monument stood on its hill, pale against a sky crowded with fast-moving clouds."},{"p":"Dad got out on the other side and immediately lost his hat."},{"p":"It skated beneath the car in front of us."},{"p":"Mom laughed so hard she had to hold the door."},{"p":"“Very funny,” Dad said, kneeling to reach for it."},{"p":"I looked at my phone. The wind forecast had been right, though that didn’t make the hat any easier to retrieve."},{"p":"Uncle Everett waited near the visitor area with a paper cup of coffee. He wore sunglasses and a shirt with a faded navy-blue collar. His hair didn’t move much in the wind. There wasn’t enough of it."},{"p":"“You’re late,” he said."},{"p":"“Traffic,” Dad replied."},{"p":"“Hat trouble,” Mom added."},{"p":"Everett glanced at Dad’s dusty knees. “I see.”"},{"p":"“Are we going up the hill first?” I asked."},{"p":"“Good morning to you too,” Everett said."},{"p":"“Morning. Hill?”"},{"p":"He tipped his cup toward the visitor center. “I was thinking we’d start inside.”"},{"p":"“But the wind’s picking up.”"},{"p":"“Which is why we might enjoy a few minutes indoors.”"},{"p":"“The monument’s outside.”"},{"p":"“Remarkable observation.”"},{"p":"I looked to Dad for support. He was brushing sand off his hat and pretending not to hear."},{"p":"Inside, the air-conditioning made the sweat on my back feel cold. People clustered around exhibits and old photographs. A little boy in a dinosaur shirt kept asking his father whether the airplane had rockets. His father said no four times before they moved on."},{"p":"I stopped in front of an image of the 1903 Flyer."},{"p":"It was stranger than I’d remembered. The wings looked like shelves covered in fabric. There wasn’t a cockpit, not really. The pilot lay facedown on the lower wing, out in the open, where the wind could get at everything."},{"p":"“That’s what they flew?” I asked."},{"p":"Everett came up beside me. “That’s the machine.”"},{"p":"“It looks fragile.”"},{"p":"“It was carefully built. Fragile isn’t quite the same as careless.”"},{"p":"I leaned closer to the photograph. “I wouldn’t want to fly lying down.”"},{"p":"“Neither would I.”"},{"p":"“You’d still do it.”"},{"p":"“Not without knowing a lot more about it.”"},{"p":"I turned to him. “You flew a Tomcat off a carrier.”"},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“So you wouldn’t be scared of this.”"},{"p":"He lowered his coffee cup. “I didn’t say scared. I said I wouldn’t climb into an unfamiliar airplane without understanding it.”"},{"p":"There it was again: the part where my uncle turned an interesting conversation into something about being careful."},{"p":"“Fine,” I said."},{"p":"Everett looked at me for a second, then wandered toward another display."},{"p":"I stayed where I was."},{"p":"The photograph showed a machine that had barely left the ground. Its pilot wasn’t smiling. He had his hands full."},{"p":"I looked for the controls. There were wires, supports, and a framework that didn’t resemble the cockpit I’d sat in with Mara. I could identify a few parts, but most of the arrangement was beyond me."},{"p":"A man nearby was telling his daughter that the Wright brothers had figured everything out on their first try. I knew that wasn’t true. They’d spent years experimenting with gliders, testing wing shapes, and working out how to control a flying machine. I almost corrected him. Then I remembered how it felt when Beckett corrected me in front of everyone."},{"p":"The daughter had already moved on to the next display."},{"p":"I turned back to the Flyer."},{"brk":1},{"p":"We climbed the hill after lunch."},{"p":"By then the clouds had broken into long, ragged strips, and sunlight flashed over the grass whenever a gap passed overhead. The wind pushed at my shirt from the ocean side. I could smell salt and hot pavement and the sunscreen Mom had insisted I put on twice."},{"p":"Dad climbed slowly, pausing halfway to pretend he was studying a sign."},{"p":"“It’s a hill,” I told him."},{"p":"“Very observant.”"},{"p":"“It’s not even that big.”"},{"p":"“Then you won’t mind carrying me the rest of the way.”"},{"p":"Mom passed us without stopping. “Neither of you is funny.”"},{"p":"At the top, I could see the flat ground stretching toward the markers for the first flights. Cars moved along roads beyond the memorial. In the distance, the ocean was a strip of darker blue under the clouds."},{"p":"Everett stood beside me, hands in his pockets."},{"p":"“You were quiet inside,” he said."},{"p":"“I was reading.”"},{"p":"“Good habit.”"},{"p":"I scraped the toe of my sneaker against the path. Fine grit gathered around the rubber."},{"p":"“Do you think the Wright brothers knew they’d succeed?”"},{"p":"“Depends what you mean by succeed.”"},{"p":"“Get off the ground.”"},{"p":"“They had evidence their ideas were working. They also had problems they hadn’t solved.”"},{"p":"“That’s not really an answer.”"},{"p":"“No,” he said. “It isn’t.”"},{"p":"He waited, but I wasn’t going to rescue the conversation for him."},{"p":"Down below, a group of kids ran toward the flight markers. One had a red jacket tied around his waist that flapped behind him like a flag."},{"p":"“Twelve seconds,” I said."},{"p":"Everett nodded."},{"p":"“That’s shorter than it takes to microwave leftovers.”"},{"p":"“Depends on the leftovers.”"},{"p":"I ignored that. “They worked for years for twelve seconds.”"},{"p":"“Among other things.”"},{"p":"“Wouldn’t you be disappointed?”"},{"p":"Everett took his sunglasses off and wiped one lens with the edge of his shirt. “I expect they were more interested in what the flight proved than how long it lasted.”"},{"p":"I watched the kids race between the markers."},{"p":"“What did it prove?”"},{"p":"“That they could do it. And that they had more work to do.”"},{"p":"He put his sunglasses back on. No grand speech followed. He was watching a man try to take a family picture while the wind blew everyone’s hair across their faces."},{"p":"I thought about Mara telling me to ease my grip. I had heard her. I’d even repeated the instruction. Then I’d grabbed the yoke as if it were the only thing keeping the airplane in the sky."},{"p":"“Can I ask you something?” I said."},{"p":"“Apparently.”"},{"p":"“When you switched from the Tomcat to the Super Hornet, were you bad at it at first?”"},{"p":"He gave me a sideways look. “What do you mean by bad?”"},{"p":"“Not as good as you thought you’d be.”"},{"p":"Everett’s mouth tightened. For a second I thought he wasn’t going to answer."},{"p":"“I was used to knowing where everything was,” he said. “I had habits that worked in one airplane and didn’t belong in the other. That was frustrating.”"},{"p":"“Did anybody notice?”"},{"p":"“Everyone was learning. Of course they noticed.”"},{"p":"“Did you hate that?”"},{"p":"“Some days.”"},{"p":"He didn’t smile, and I liked him better for it."},{"p":"Mom called us over for a photograph. Dad had his hat pressed down with one hand. The wind was blowing Mom’s hair across her mouth, and she kept trying to tuck it behind her ear while holding the phone."},{"p":"“Stand closer,” she said."},{"p":"I moved an inch."},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"I moved another inch."},{"p":"Everett hooked an arm around my shoulders and pulled me into place."},{"p":"“Smile,” Mom said."},{"p":"The wind hit us hard enough to make my eyes water. Dad’s hat came off again. Mom took the picture anyway."}]},{"t":"Chapter Thirteen — The Wind Had Other Plans","blocks":[{"p":"By the time we got back to the car, Dad had sand in his shoes and Mom had decided she was never letting him choose a parking spot again."},{"p":"“It was the closest space,” he said."},{"p":"“To a puddle,” Mom answered."},{"p":"“It wasn’t a puddle when we parked.”"},{"p":"I looked back toward the memorial. The hill stood against a low gray sky, and people were still climbing it, shoulders bent into the wind. I wanted to stay. Not because there was something else I needed to see. I just wasn’t finished looking."},{"p":"Everett caught me hanging behind. “You coming?”"},{"p":"“In a second.”"},{"p":"He waited beside the car, which made it difficult to pretend I was alone."},{"p":"“You know what I thought it’d be like?” I asked."},{"p":"“The memorial?”"},{"p":"“The first flight. I thought there’d be more to it.”"},{"p":"“There was quite a bit to it.”"},{"p":"“I mean the actual flying. Twelve seconds. If I counted slowly, I’d be done.”"},{"p":"Everett pulled at the cuff of his jacket. “They weren’t trying to impress you.”"},{"p":"“I’m aware of that.”"},{"p":"“Good.”"},{"p":"He opened the car door and ducked inside. The conversation was apparently over."},{"p":"I climbed in after him, irritated by how easily he could stop talking when I was still trying to figure out what I meant."},{"p":"We’d planned to walk along the beach before dinner. Mom had packed towels and a bag of snacks, and Dad had promised to show me the place where he said he’d once found a shark’s tooth. But when we reached the public access, the wind had picked up. Sand moved over the wooden walkway in little pale streams. It stung my bare ankles and found its way under the tongue of my shoes."},{"p":"“Five minutes,” Mom announced. “Then we decide.”"},{"p":"Dad stood on the beach with his arms spread, facing the ocean like a man advertising weatherproof jackets."},{"p":"“This is invigorating!” he called."},{"p":"His hat went flying."},{"p":"Everett caught it before it reached the water. He didn’t run so much as move three very efficient steps and put out one hand. Dad looked offended by the lack of drama."},{"p":"I watched the gulls. Some seemed to hang above the dunes, wings tilted, making tiny adjustments I couldn’t follow. One dropped toward the sand, changed its mind, and slid sideways without much effort."},{"p":"“Look at that,” I said."},{"p":"Mom was trying to keep her hair out of her mouth. “At what?”"},{"p":"“The birds.”"},{"p":"“They’re probably wishing they had a car.”"},{"p":"“No, watch. That one’s not even flapping.”"},{"p":"Everett came up beside me, holding Dad’s hat by the brim. “What do you think it’s doing?”"},{"p":"“Using the wind.”"},{"p":"“How?”"},{"p":"I watched a gull tilt its wings. “I don’t know. It’s adjusting.”"},{"p":"“That’s a start.”"},{"p":"I turned to him. “You could tell me.”"},{"p":"“I could. But you noticed something worth looking at. Keep looking.”"},{"p":"That annoyed me almost as much as the sand. Still, I watched. The bird wasn’t motionless. It rocked, tilted a wing, and steadied itself again."},{"p":"On my first flight, every little change in the airplane had felt like a problem I needed to fix immediately. I remembered Mara saying, *Ease up. Give it a second.* I’d heard her. I’d just been too busy squeezing the yoke to try it."},{"p":"The gull banked toward the dunes and vanished behind the grass."},{"p":"Dad came over and reclaimed his hat. “We voting on dinner or frostbite?”"},{"p":"“Dinner,” Mom said."},{"p":"“I didn’t get a vote,” I complained."},{"p":"“You can vote in the car.”"},{"p":"We made it to the walkway before the rain began. Not a proper downpour, just cold drops carried sideways that made the sand stick to my shins. Dad tried to shake the water out of his hat and sprayed Mom."},{"p":"She stopped walking."},{"p":"He stopped too."},{"p":"“Sorry,” he said."},{"p":"“You’re driving.”"},{"p":"“I was already driving.”"},{"p":"“Then you’re driving quietly.”"},{"p":"Everett bent his head so they wouldn’t see him smile."},{"p":"At dinner, I took the notebook out of my backpack. I’d brought it because I thought I might need to record something important. The pages smelled faintly of pencil shavings and the orange I’d left in the same pocket."},{"p":"Across the table, Dad was attempting to cut a piece of fish with a plastic knife that had bent nearly in half. Mom traded him hers without a word."},{"p":"I drew a bird and an arrow for the wind. It looked like a potato with feathers, but I knew what I meant."},{"p":"Underneath I wrote: *Small corrections. Don’t fight every bump.*"},{"p":"Everett glanced over. “Homework?”"},{"p":"“Not for school.”"},{"p":"He nodded and went back to his food."},{"p":"I waited for him to say something about the note. He didn’t. After a while I slid the notebook under my plate so the corner wouldn’t curl in the damp air."}]},{"t":"Chapter Fourteen — A Question I Didn’t Want to Ask","blocks":[{"p":"On Monday, the academy room smelled of dry-erase markers and the burnt-toast odor from the break-room microwave. Somebody had left the door open to the hallway, and every few minutes a gust carried in the noise of a cart rattling across the concrete outside."},{"p":"I arrived early enough to choose a seat. I liked getting there first, although I wouldn’t have admitted it. The back corner was too far from the board. The front row felt like volunteering to be embarrassed. I picked the middle."},{"p":"Imani came in with a folded weather printout under one arm. She put it on the table and slid into the chair beside me."},{"p":"“Trip good?” she asked."},{"p":"“Yeah. Windy.”"},{"p":"She gave me a look. “At the Outer Banks? Shocking.”"},{"p":"“I saw the flight markers.”"},{"p":"“Were they as far apart as you thought?”"},{"p":"“No.” I pulled out my notebook. “Actually, that was the weird part.”"},{"p":"I showed her the drawing of the gull. She leaned over it, squinted, and turned the notebook sideways."},{"p":"“Is that a bird?”"},{"p":"“Obviously.”"},{"p":"“I thought it was a shoe.”"},{"p":"“It’s a bird.”"},{"p":"“Okay.” She traced the arrow with her finger. “What’s this?”"},{"p":"“The wind.”"},{"p":"“And the shoe?”"},{"p":"I shut the notebook."},{"p":"Imani laughed. Not meanly, either. That was the worst part. I’d been ready for an argument."},{"p":"Beckett arrived carrying a cardboard box with a wing sticking out. He had built another glider, this one from foam board, and was explaining a change to its tail before he’d even set the box down. Rafe followed him, eating something out of a paper bag."},{"p":"“You know,” Rafe said, “most people say hello first.”"},{"p":"“Hello,” Beckett replied. “I changed the tail.”"},{"p":"Mara came in while Beckett was demonstrating how the model balanced on two fingers. She watched him for a moment, then pointed to the box."},{"p":"“That staying inside today?”"},{"p":"“Unless we can test it.”"},{"p":"“Inside,” she said."},{"p":"Beckett lowered the wing with obvious disappointment."},{"p":"Mara wrote three words on the board: *Look. Think. Ask.*"},{"p":"“I want to go over your last flight experiences,” she said. “Not what went well. One thing you didn’t understand.”"},{"p":"I looked down at my hands. There was a faint pencil mark along the side of my thumb from the notebook. I rubbed it with my other thumb until it smudged."},{"p":"Rafe went first. He said he’d been surprised by how difficult it was to hear a radio call when an instructor was speaking at the same time. Mara asked what he’d done. He said he’d pretended to understand the call, then admitted it to his instructor afterward."},{"p":"“That didn’t help much at the time,” Mara said."},{"p":"“No,” Rafe answered. “It did not.”"},{"p":"Beckett wanted to know why a particular gauge had looked different from the diagram in his book. Mara asked which diagram and made him bring it up on his tablet. Imani talked about watching clouds change during a ground session and realizing she couldn’t tell whether they were getting lower or she was just paying more attention."},{"p":"Then Mara looked at me."},{"p":"“Lincoln?”"},{"p":"I had an answer ready about airspeed. It sounded respectable and wouldn’t require me to mention my white-knuckled grip on the yoke."},{"p":"But I could still feel the airplane wandering when I tried to hold it straight. I could hear myself saying *I’ve got it* before I actually did."},{"p":"“I didn’t know when to stop correcting,” I said."},{"p":"Mara put the marker down. “Tell me what you mean.”"},{"p":"“When the nose moved a little, I moved the yoke. Then it moved the other way, so I moved it back. And I kept doing that.”"},{"p":"“Did the airplane need all those corrections?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“How do you know?”"},{"p":"“Because you told me.”"},{"p":"A few people laughed. I felt heat climb my neck."},{"p":"Mara didn’t laugh. “That’s one reason. What else?”"},{"p":"I thought of the gull over the beach, how it had made adjustments without looking as if it were fighting anything. But a bird wasn’t an airplane, and I didn’t want to sound stupid."},{"p":"“I think I was watching every little movement instead of looking where I wanted to go.”"},{"p":"“That’s worth working on,” she said. “Small inputs. Give the airplane time to respond. And keep your eyes outside when it’s appropriate. The instruments support what you’re doing; they don’t replace looking out the window.”"},{"p":"I wrote it down. I’d known the words before my first flight, too."},{"p":"After class, Mara asked me to stay for a minute. The others moved into the hallway, Beckett’s box scraping the doorframe on its way out."},{"p":"She waited until the room was quiet."},{"p":"“You looked uncomfortable talking about that flight.”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"She stacked the handouts without pressing. Somehow that was worse."},{"p":"“I thought I’d be better,” I said."},{"p":"“Better than what?”"},{"p":"“Than I was.”"},{"p":"She straightened the pile against the table. “You were on your first lesson.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Do you?”"},{"p":"I didn’t answer."},{"p":"Mara pulled out a chair and sat, leaving the other one empty. I stayed standing."},{"p":"“I can’t promise every lesson will feel good,” she said. “Some days we’ll stay on the ground. Some days we’ll repeat something you’ve already done. And if you don’t understand an instruction, I need you to say so. I can’t teach the version of you that’s trying to look experienced.”"},{"p":"I stared at the edge of the table. A strip of clear tape had been stuck there and peeled away, leaving a tacky patch that caught dust."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said."},{"p":"She didn’t let me off with that."},{"p":"“What are you going to do differently next time?”"},{"p":"I opened my notebook, found the page with the gull, and turned it toward her. She studied the drawing without saying anything."},{"p":"“It’s a bird,” I said, before she could ask."},{"p":"“I got that far.”"},{"p":"I pointed to the note underneath. “I watched them in the wind. They kept adjusting, but they weren’t—” I moved my hand back and forth too quickly, the way I’d moved the yoke. “They weren’t doing that.”"},{"p":"Mara looked at the page a little longer."},{"p":"“An airplane isn’t a bird,” she said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“But noticing how you respond to movement is useful.” She tapped the words I’d written. “On the next lesson, we’ll give you something specific to practice.”"},{"p":"“What?”"},{"p":"“Straight-and-level flight. A steady outside reference. Small corrections.”"},{"p":"“That’s it?”"},{"p":"“That’s plenty.”"},{"p":"I closed the notebook. The tape on the table stuck briefly to the sleeve of my sweatshirt as I stood to leave."},{"p":"At the doorway I stopped."},{"p":"“Can I ask one more thing?”"},{"p":"Mara was already wiping the board. “Go ahead.”"},{"p":"“If I’m not sure whether I’m doing something right, should I say it right away? Even if you’re already watching?”"},{"p":"She turned around, marker eraser in her hand."},{"p":"“Yes,” she said. “Especially then.”"}]},{"t":"Chapter Fifteen — The Far End of the Runway","blocks":[{"p":"The second time I went flying, I almost left my notebook at home."},{"p":"It was sitting on the kitchen counter under a grocery receipt, exactly where I’d put it so I wouldn’t forget it. I had already buckled my seat belt when Mom came out of the house holding it by one corner."},{"p":"“Important?” she asked through the open window."},{"p":"“Sort of.”"},{"p":"She handed it in. The cover had a greasy fingerprint across the drawing of a gull."},{"p":"“Then maybe put it somewhere you can remember.”"},{"p":"“I did.”"},{"p":"Mom looked toward the kitchen window, then back at me. “Clearly.”"},{"p":"She went around to the driver’s side. I tucked the notebook into my backpack, between my water bottle and a packet of crackers that had been crushed into approximately a thousand pieces."},{"p":"I hadn’t told anybody I was nervous. I’d spent the whole week complaining that I wasn’t getting enough time in an airplane."},{"p":"The road to Hampton Roads Executive Airport ran past a gas station with a broken price sign. Every few seconds, the loose plastic number slapped against its frame. Mom turned the radio down as we passed it."},{"p":"“Did you sleep?” she asked."},{"p":"“Some.”"},{"p":"“That’s not a number.”"},{"p":"“Six hours. Maybe.”"},{"p":"She glanced over. “Did you stay up watching flying videos?”"},{"p":"I watched a pickup truck pull out of a side street. “One.”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“Three. But one was short.”"},{"p":"Mom sighed through her nose. “Next time, sleep is part of getting ready.”"},{"p":"I wanted to argue that I was ready. My stomach had been doing little flips since breakfast, though, so I let it go."},{"p":"At the airport, the chain-link fence was wet with dew. A maintenance cart rattled past the hangars, its driver wearing enormous ear protectors and carrying a paper cup between two fingers. The air smelled of cut grass and fuel, with a faint muddy smell from the ditch beyond the parking lot."},{"p":"Mara was at the briefing table, writing numbers on a sheet of paper. She looked up when I came in."},{"p":"“You’re early.”"},{"p":"“Mom drives fast.”"},{"p":"From behind me, Mom said, “I drive the speed limit.”"},{"p":"“Mostly,” I said."},{"p":"Mara gave Mom a quick smile. “We’ll be reviewing conditions first. If everything is suitable, we’ll do a local lesson.”"},{"p":"That *if* bothered me. It bothered me less than it had a month earlier, which I supposed counted as progress."},{"p":"Mom left after confirming the pickup time. Mara turned the weather sheet so I could see it."},{"p":"“What do you notice?”"},{"p":"I looked for a number I recognized. “Wind is lighter than last time.”"},{"p":"“Good. Anything else?”"},{"p":"I read the visibility and cloud information aloud, slowly enough not to stumble over the abbreviations. Mara asked what the numbers meant instead of letting me get away with reading them aloud. When I guessed at one, she put her pencil down."},{"p":"“Try that again.”"},{"p":"“I don’t know that one.”"},{"p":"“Good. Now we can work with it.”"},{"p":"She didn’t act surprised. She just showed me where to find the answer."},{"p":"Outside, the Cessna sat in the morning light with its tie-down ropes still attached. Its white paint had a thin gray streak below one window. Mara handed me the checklist and walked beside me while I worked through it. I read each item aloud, touched what I was checking, and waited when something didn’t make sense. At one point I reached for a latch, hesitated, and asked her to show me what properly secured should look and feel like."},{"p":"She did. No speech about responsibility. Just the latch, her hand, and the difference between a loose rattle and a firm click."},{"p":"By the time we climbed aboard, sweat had collected under my shirt collar. I put on the headset, adjusted the seat with Mara’s help, and checked that I could reach the controls comfortably. The cockpit smelled of warm vinyl and a little dust. Somewhere behind my elbow, the metal buckle on a spare belt knocked softly against the sidewall."},{"p":"Mara handled the departure, talking me through what she was doing. I followed the checklist and listened to her radio calls, trying not to stare at the runway as it came closer. I had imagined the takeoff all week, but the real thing was mostly noise, vibration, and the strange feeling of my body becoming heavier against the seat before the wheels left the pavement."},{"p":"Once we were clear of the airport traffic area and established at a suitable altitude for the lesson, she leveled the airplane and glanced at me."},{"p":"“Your controls.”"},{"p":"“My controls,” I answered."},{"p":"I placed my hands on the yoke. The pressure felt lighter than I’d remembered."},{"p":"“Pick something well ahead of us,” she said. “Not the nearest road. Farther.”"},{"p":"I chose a pale break in the tree line where a river curved toward the distance."},{"p":"“Tell me what you see.”"},{"p":"“The river. A water tower left of it. That cloud looks like a potato.”"},{"p":"“Useful observation. We’ll leave the potato out of the flight log.”"},{"p":"I grinned, then noticed the nose had crept a little above my reference. My first instinct was to push forward. I did, too sharply. The nose dipped."},{"p":"“Small,” Mara reminded me."},{"p":"I eased the pressure. Then I eased it again because I was still doing too much."},{"p":"The nose didn’t settle immediately. I had to stop myself from adding another correction before I could see what the first one had done."},{"p":"“Look outside,” she said. “Don’t chase the needle.”"},{"p":"I found the river again. For a little while, I could keep the picture in front of me nearly the same. The engine made its steady rough hum, the headset squeezed my ears, and sunlight crawled across the top of the instrument panel as we moved."},{"p":"Then a bump lifted my stomach."},{"p":"My fingers tightened."},{"p":"The yoke moved."},{"p":"The airplane answered."},{"p":"I tried to fix the answer, and made another problem."},{"p":"“I’m doing it again,” I said."},{"p":"“You are.”"},{"p":"“Can you—” I swallowed. “Can you take it for a second?”"},{"p":"“My controls.”"},{"p":"“Your controls.”"},{"p":"Mara took over. The airplane steadied while I pulled my hands into my lap. My palms were damp enough to leave dark marks on my jeans."},{"p":"“I had the airplane,” she said. “Tell me what started it.”"},{"p":"“The bump.”"},{"p":"“And then?”"},{"p":"“I grabbed the yoke like it was trying to get away.”"},{"p":"“That sounds about right.”"},{"p":"I waited for the rest of the correction. She just held the airplane steady and let me look out the window."},{"p":"Below us, a cluster of houses sat between strips of trees. From up here the roads looked tidy, even though I knew perfectly well there were potholes down there, and stoplights, and people who forgot to use turn signals."},{"p":"“Want another try?” she asked."},{"p":"I did. I also didn’t."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Say it like you mean it.”"},{"p":"“Yes. I want another try.”"},{"p":"She nodded. “Your controls.”"},{"p":"“My controls.”"},{"p":"This time I rested my hands lightly. I watched the far-off river instead of the instrument needles. When the nose drifted, I gave the yoke the smallest pressure I could manage and waited to see what happened."},{"p":"It wasn’t perfect. We wandered a little. Once Mara had me check the instruments to confirm what my eyes suggested, and I realized I’d been letting our altitude creep away while concentrating on direction."},{"p":"“Two things at once,” I muttered."},{"p":"“Eventually more than two,” she said."},{"p":"“That seems unnecessary.”"},{"p":"She laughed, but only briefly. “Check your reference again.”"},{"p":"I did."},{"p":"We stayed up long enough for the lesson to feel like work. My shoulders ached from holding them too high. I made fewer large corrections, though, and when Mara pointed out something I hadn’t noticed, I didn’t feel compelled to explain why I’d missed it."},{"p":"On the way back, she took the controls for the arrival and landing. I watched her scan between the outside view and the instruments, listened to the radio, and kept my hands out of the way. The runway grew wider through the windshield. A little crosswind pushed against the airplane, and Mara made adjustments that were almost invisible from my seat."},{"p":"After we’d parked, completed the shutdown procedures, and secured the airplane, she asked me to carry my notebook inside."},{"p":"I opened it at the briefing table, ready to write down everything she’d said."},{"p":"“Before you do that,” Mara said, “what went better?”"},{"p":"I rubbed the pencil eraser against my thumb. “I didn’t fight it as much.”"},{"p":"“What didn’t go well?”"},{"p":"“I still tried to fix a bump before I knew what the airplane was doing.”"},{"p":"She nodded. “And?”"},{"p":"I looked at the page with the gull drawing. “I asked you to take over.”"},{"p":"“Was that a mistake?”"},{"p":"I thought about my damp palms and how badly I’d wanted to prove I could do it without help."},{"p":"“No,” I said. “I don’t think so.”"},{"p":"Mara slid the lesson sheet across the table. “Neither do I.”"},{"p":"I started writing, but my pencil point broke on the first word. The broken tip rolled into the spiral binding, where I couldn’t get it out with my fingernail."},{"p":"“Do you have another pencil?” I asked."},{"p":"Mara opened the drawer beside her. “That’s a question I can answer.”"}]},{"t":"Chapter Sixteen — The Thing About Being Right","blocks":[{"p":"Beckett had taken over the end of the academy table with a pile of cardboard, two rulers, and a roll of tape big enough to repair a boat."},{"p":"“Don’t touch anything,” he said when I walked in."},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to.”"},{"p":"“You’re standing close.”"},{"p":"“It’s a table, Beckett. People stand near them.”"},{"p":"He moved a cardboard wing out of my reach anyway."},{"p":"The academy was holding a small Saturday workshop on balance and loading. Mara had brought a training weight-and-balance example for us to work through, while Beckett had arrived with a model he claimed would make the whole subject easier. Imani sat across from him with a calculator and three sharpened pencils lined up beside her notebook. Rafe was trying to make one of the cardboard scraps into a hat."},{"p":"“That’s a terrible hat,” I told him."},{"p":"“It’s aerodynamic.”"},{"p":"“It’s square.”"},{"p":"“Less wind resistance than my hair.”"},{"p":"His curls escaped from under the cardboard. Imani didn’t look up."},{"p":"“Please don’t wear it when Mara comes in.”"},{"p":"“Why?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“Because she’ll make it part of the lesson.”"},{"p":"He took it off."},{"p":"Mara arrived carrying a plastic tub of small weights. She set it on the table with a clatter."},{"p":"“Today,” she said, “we’re going to find out why an airplane can be within its maximum weight and still be loaded incorrectly.”"},{"p":"Beckett raised his hand before she finished speaking."},{"p":"“Because the center of gravity can be outside limits.”"},{"p":"“Yes,” Mara said. “And since you’ve given away the answer, you can help us show why.”"},{"p":"He looked pleased until she handed him the tub."},{"p":"“Set up your model.”"},{"p":"His smile thinned. “It’s not completely finished.”"},{"p":"“Then we’ll learn from the unfinished version.”"},{"p":"He glanced at me, as though this was somehow my fault."},{"p":"We used a cardboard shape balanced on a rounded wooden support, then moved small weights to different positions along it. Mara reminded us that cardboard couldn’t predict how an airplane would fly. It could, however, show how moving a weight shifted the balance point."},{"p":"With the weights clustered near the middle, the cardboard stayed level. When Rafe moved several pieces toward the tail, the nose rose."},{"p":"“That’s dramatic,” he said."},{"p":"“It’s cardboard,” Imani replied. “It has no shame.”"},{"p":"Mara handed us a sample loading worksheet from a fictional training flight. It included occupants, baggage, fuel, and reference data for an example aircraft. We had to add the weights, multiply each by its assigned arm to find its moment, then divide the total moment by the total weight to locate the center of gravity. Finally, we had to check both weight and balance against the example airplane’s limits."},{"p":"The arithmetic took longer than the demonstration."},{"p":"I finished first and circled my answer so hard the pencil nearly tore the page."},{"p":"“Done.”"},{"p":"Imani was still checking her numbers. Beckett had two columns crossed out. Rafe was counting on his fingers under the table."},{"p":"Mara came around and looked at my sheet."},{"p":"“Show me how you got this.”"},{"p":"I pointed to the last line."},{"p":"“That’s your answer. I asked how you got it.”"},{"p":"I turned the paper toward her. “I multiplied the weights by the arms and added them up.”"},{"p":"“And this number?”"},{"p":"“Total weight.”"},{"p":"She tapped the margin. “You copied the baggage weight into the fuel row.”"},{"p":"I stared. The two numbers weren’t even particularly similar."},{"p":"“Oh.”"},{"p":"“Try it again.”"},{"p":"Beckett leaned over. “That’s why you label the columns.”"},{"p":"“I labeled them.”"},{"p":"“You labeled one ‘stuff.’”"},{"p":"“Because it was stuff.”"},{"p":"“That’s not a unit.”"},{"p":"I turned to Mara. “Can I work somewhere else?”"},{"p":"“You can work at this table and check your numbers.”"},{"p":"I erased the calculation until the paper turned gray. Beckett made the noise he made when he was trying not to say something."},{"p":"“Just say it,” I snapped."},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to.”"},{"p":"“You always are.”"},{"p":"He put his pencil down. “Fine. You don’t have to be first every time.”"},{"p":"Nobody spoke for a second."},{"p":"Rafe stopped folding the cardboard hat. Imani stared at her calculator."},{"p":"“I’m not trying to be first,” I said."},{"p":"Beckett looked at the enormous circle around my answer."},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"That was worse than if he’d argued."},{"p":"Mara didn’t tell us to apologize. She picked up the tub of weights and moved to the other end of the table, where she began helping Rafe understand the worksheet. The room filled with ordinary noises again: pencils tapping, a chair scraping, the air conditioner clicking on."},{"p":"I worked the numbers a second time. This time I wrote every label in full. My answer changed. It was still wrong."},{"p":"I pushed the paper away."},{"p":"Imani leaned across, not touching it. “Can I show you where I got stuck?”"},{"p":"“You got stuck?”"},{"p":"“Twice.”"},{"p":"She turned her notebook so I could see a messy patch of erased numbers. “I mixed up the total moment and the arm. Then I did it again because I was annoyed.”"},{"p":"I compared her sheet with mine. “How’d you catch it?”"},{"p":"“I checked whether the result made sense. Mine put the balance point somewhere past the end of the airplane.”"},{"p":"I laughed before I could stop myself."},{"p":"“That’s probably bad.”"},{"p":"“Probably.”"},{"p":"She pointed to my second calculation. “I think you’ve divided by the wrong total.”"},{"p":"I checked. She was right."},{"p":"Across the table, Beckett was holding his cardboard model steady while Rafe shifted a weight along its length. He didn’t look over."},{"p":"After we finished, Mara gathered our worksheets. She reminded us that actual aircraft loading required the correct, current aircraft-specific data and procedures, not a classroom model or somebody’s guess. Then she dismissed us for lunch."},{"p":"I was stuffing my notebook into my bag when Beckett came back for his ruler."},{"p":"“Your model’s pretty good,” I said."},{"p":"He picked up the ruler. “The support needs to be smoother. It catches.”"},{"p":"“Still.”"},{"p":"He waited."},{"p":"“I was being a jerk,” I said."},{"p":"Beckett looked at the table. “You were being loud.”"},{"p":"“That’s not the same thing?”"},{"p":"“Sometimes.”"},{"p":"I zipped my bag. “You could’ve said it differently.”"},{"p":"“Probably.”"},{"p":"Neither of us moved."},{"p":"From the hallway, Rafe shouted, “If you two are done arguing about math, there are sandwiches.”"},{"p":"Beckett tucked the ruler under his arm. “He says ‘sandwiches’ like they’re going to escape.”"},{"p":"“They might, if he gets there first.”"},{"p":"We went out together, though Beckett walked half a step ahead of me, still carrying the ruler like he expected somebody to steal it."}]},{"t":"Chapter Seventeen — The Red Tag","blocks":[{"p":"The next Saturday, somebody had tied a red ribbon to the airplane."},{"p":"Not a ribbon, exactly. It was a bright fabric streamer attached to a cover on the pitot tube, and it fluttered in the breeze as though the airplane had won a prize for being parked."},{"p":"I spotted it before I spotted Mara."},{"p":"“Does that mean we’re not flying?” I asked."},{"p":"Mara was beside the hangar, trying to hold a clipboard flat against her knee while she wrote. Her ponytail had come loose. She glanced at the airplane."},{"p":"“It means there’s a pitot cover on the pitot tube.”"},{"p":"“Right. But are we flying?”"},{"p":"“Good morning to you, too.”"},{"p":"“Morning.”"},{"p":"She finished the line she was writing and capped her pen. “You tell me what the cover means.”"},{"p":"“That somebody doesn’t want bugs in the tube.”"},{"p":"“Good. What else?”"},{"p":"“That we take it off before flying.”"},{"p":"“And if we forget?”"},{"p":"I knew this one. We’d gone over it with a diagram, the one where Imani had drawn a tiny bug with a suitcase moving into the pitot tube."},{"p":"“The airspeed indication could be unreliable.”"},{"p":"“Could be. Depends on the blockage and the system. So what’s your first job today?”"},{"p":"“Take off the cover.”"},{"p":"“Your first job is to slow down enough to notice it’s there.”"},{"p":"I looked at the streamer again. It was about the brightest thing on the ramp. You’d have to work pretty hard not to notice it."},{"p":"Mara saw my face. “You’d be surprised what people miss when they’re thinking about the next thing.”"},{"p":"I glanced at the bright streamer again. Coach would have had something to say about me missing that."},{"p":"She handed me a clipboard with the aircraft’s checklist secured under the clip. We walked toward the Skyhawk. Its white paint had a dull film of pollen along the upper wing, and the black rubber tire nearest me had a tiny stone caught in the tread. A fuel truck crawled along the far side of the ramp. Somewhere inside the maintenance hangar a metal tool hit the floor, followed by a word I was fairly sure the academy didn’t teach."},{"p":"I started at the first item."},{"p":"This time, I said each step aloud without performing for Mara. I wanted her to see I could do it, obviously. I also wanted to avoid that particular expression she wore when she realized I was guessing."},{"p":"At the nose, I checked the propeller with my eyes and followed her directions for the inspection. She stayed close, correcting how I positioned myself around it. We looked at the cowling, the openings, the landing gear. I didn’t touch anything she hadn’t cleared me to touch."},{"p":"When we reached the left wing, I stopped."},{"p":"A narrow dark streak marked the underside near an inspection point. It might have been grime. It might not. I bent closer, careful not to brush against it."},{"p":"Mara waited."},{"p":"“Is that supposed to be there?” I asked."},{"p":"“What do you think it is?”"},{"p":"“I don’t know.”"},{"p":"She crouched beside me. “That’s an acceptable answer.”"},{"p":"“Doesn’t seem very useful.”"},{"p":"“It’s useful if it’s true.”"},{"p":"She studied the mark, then went inside to check the airplane’s maintenance records with the flight school staff. I followed her back toward the office. Through the glass I could see Rafe balancing a paper cup on the edge of a chair and Beckett gesturing at something with both hands. The cup tipped. Rafe caught it against his shirt. I wished I’d been there to see the whole thing."},{"p":"At the counter, Mara spoke quietly with the mechanic. I could hear words, not the whole conversation. The mechanic went out to look at the airplane himself."},{"p":"I stared through the window. We’d had a canceled flight already. I had spent the entire week replaying the last one, especially the part where my hand kept moving too much and the nose kept answering every movement like a dog pulling at a leash. I’d practiced with a pencil balanced across my palm, pretending it was the control yoke. Dad had found me doing it during dinner and asked if we needed to buy the pencil its own seat."},{"p":"The mechanic returned after several minutes and spoke with Mara. The school logged the discrepancy for further evaluation. I didn’t try to read the screen over his shoulder."},{"p":"Mara joined me by the window."},{"p":"“We’re not flying that airplane until the discrepancy has been evaluated and properly resolved,” she said."},{"p":"I looked out at the Skyhawk. “Because of that little mark?”"},{"p":"“Because it needs to be evaluated and cleared properly. A small mark can have a small explanation. It can also be a reason to investigate.”"},{"p":"“But we were supposed to fly.”"},{"p":"“We were.”"},{"p":"“Can we use another airplane?”"},{"p":"“Not today. The available one has a different equipment setup, and we haven’t prepared you for it.”"},{"p":"I shoved my hands into my pockets. The seam inside the right pocket had split, and my thumb found the hole automatically."},{"p":"Mara waited for whatever I was going to say next. I didn’t know what it was yet. Something rude was gathering behind my teeth, though, so I kept them together."},{"p":"Through the glass, I watched the streamer flick against the wing."},{"p":"“Could I have just not said anything?” I asked."},{"p":"Mara’s expression sharpened. “You could have.”"},{"p":"I looked at her."},{"p":"“And then we’d have missed the chance to check something that needed checking,” she said. “You don’t get to trade that away because you want to fly.”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Do you believe me?”"},{"p":"I thought about lying. The answer was almost ready."},{"p":"“Mostly,” I said."},{"p":"She nodded once. “I’ll take mostly. For now.”"},{"p":"Beckett pushed through the office door carrying the cup Rafe had nearly dropped. A wet stain spread across the front of Rafe’s shirt behind him."},{"p":"“You flying?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Something needs checking.”"},{"p":"“On the airplane?”"},{"p":"“No, on the moon.”"},{"p":"Beckett stopped. Rafe, still blotting his shirt with a napkin, made a choking noise."},{"p":"I felt awful immediately. Not awful enough to say it right away."},{"p":"“Sorry,” I muttered. “Yeah. On the airplane.”"},{"p":"Beckett looked past me toward the ramp. “What happened?”"},{"p":"“I saw a mark.”"},{"p":"“Where?”"},{"p":"I showed him from the window. He leaned forward, interested in a way that made me want to shake him."},{"p":"“Good catch,” he said."},{"p":"“It’s not a catch. I lost my flight.”"},{"p":"He looked at me, puzzled. “You found something worth checking.”"},{"p":"Beckett kept looking at the airplane. I looked at the empty space where my name had been on the schedule."},{"p":"Mara came out with a stack of laminated cards. “Change of plans. We’re doing a scenario exercise.”"},{"p":"Rafe groaned. “Does the scenario involve a towel?”"},{"p":"“It might,” Mara said, looking at his shirt. “But not for the airplane.”"},{"p":"She led us into the classroom. I took the chair by the window and sat there while she put three cards on the table. Each described a different flight that looked easy until one detail changed: a forecast that had worsened, an instrument that didn’t agree with another, a passenger who needed to get somewhere urgently."},{"p":"We weren’t asked to diagnose anything or fix an airplane. We had to decide what information we’d need and whether we would continue, delay, or stop."},{"p":"Rafe wanted to make a decision on every card in ten seconds. Beckett wanted to examine every number. Imani asked what the pilot actually knew, which turned out to be a much better question than either of theirs."},{"p":"On the third card, I finally said, “I wouldn’t go yet.”"},{"p":"“Why?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“Because I don’t know enough.”"},{"p":"She tapped the card with one finger. “What would you need?”"},{"p":"I began making a list."},{"p":"By the time we finished, the sun had shifted across the classroom floor. The strip of light that had been touching my shoe was halfway up the wall."},{"p":"I still wanted to fly. That hadn’t improved at all."},{"p":"But when Mara asked us to hand in our answers, I found myself writing one more question at the bottom of the last card. I wasn’t sure it was a good one. I left it there anyway."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eighteen — The Long Way Around","blocks":[{"p":"At the bottom of my card, I’d written: *Who gets to say no?*"},{"p":"Mara returned our cards on Tuesday afternoon. Beside my question, she’d written: *Anyone who notices a safety concern should speak up. The pilot in command has final responsibility for the flight, but that doesn’t mean everyone else must stay silent.*"},{"p":"I read it twice while Rafe tried to flick a rubber band into an empty trash can."},{"p":"“What’s yours say?” he asked."},{"p":"“Nothing.”"},{"p":"“Mine says I need to explain my answers.”"},{"p":"“That’s because you wrote ‘probably fine’ on all three.”"},{"p":"“Two,” he said. “The other one said ‘ask somebody.’”"},{"p":"He flicked the rubber band. It hit the wall and dropped behind the trash can."},{"p":"“Precision sport,” Beckett said without looking up."},{"p":"Mara called us to the front table, where she’d spread out a sectional chart under clear plastic. There were pencils, rulers, and a little model airplane whose left wing had been repaired with tape."},{"p":"“Today we’re going to plan a pretend trip,” she said. “Nobody’s flying it. Nobody’s filing anything. We’re learning how to ask sensible questions before an airplane leaves the ground.”"},{"p":"Rafe put his hand up. “Can the pretend trip go somewhere with pizza?”"},{"p":"“Only if you can explain the airspace.”"},{"p":"He lowered his hand."},{"p":"The exercise began at Hampton Roads Executive Airport. Mara had marked a destination on the chart and drawn a wide circle around a patch of airspace she wanted us to recognize. We had to identify landmarks, notice nearby airports, and talk through why a direct line on paper wasn’t automatically the best route."},{"p":"I drew my line immediately."},{"p":"Beckett leaned over. “You went right through the thing she circled.”"},{"p":"“I know. It’s a straight line.”"},{"p":"“That doesn’t make it a good line.”"},{"p":"“It makes it the shortest.”"},{"p":"Imani turned her chart sideways and looked at it from the other end of the table. “What altitude are you planning?”"},{"p":"I glanced at my paper. I hadn’t written one."},{"p":"“It’s pretend,” I said."},{"p":"“It’s still supposed to be a plan.”"},{"p":"“Okay. Then what altitude would you use?”"},{"p":"She pointed to the chart legend and the airspace boundaries. “First I’d figure out what the markings mean.”"},{"p":"I wanted to tell her I knew what they meant. I knew some of them. That was the problem."},{"p":"Mara came around the table and looked at our lines. She didn’t say which was best. She asked where we’d get current information, what conditions might change our choices, and what we would do if the destination became unsuitable."},{"p":"Rafe said we’d land somewhere else."},{"p":"“Where?” Mara asked."},{"p":"He pointed at the nearest airport symbol. “There.”"},{"p":"“What do you know about it?”"},{"p":"“It’s an airport.”"},{"p":"“That’s a start.”"},{"p":"By the end of the exercise, my chart looked as if a small animal had walked through a spilled pencil box. I’d erased my first line and drawn another. Then a third. Beckett had written a neat list of questions down the side of his page. Imani had put a small question mark beside every assumption she wanted to check."},{"p":"Mara leaned against the front table. “Here’s the part I want you to remember. A plan isn’t proof that everything will happen the way you expect. It’s preparation for making choices when it doesn’t.”"},{"p":"Rafe raised his hand again."},{"p":"“Yes?”"},{"p":"“Can we still go for pizza?”"},{"p":"“Ask your parents.”"},{"p":"He looked personally betrayed."},{"p":"After class, I stood beside Imani while she rolled up her chart. The paper kept trying to spring open."},{"p":"“Hey,” I said. “The thing you asked about altitude?”"},{"p":"“What about it?”"},{"p":"“I should’ve thought of that.”"},{"p":"She tucked the chart under one arm. “Yeah.”"},{"p":"I waited for something encouraging. It didn’t come."},{"p":"“Did you already know the answer?” I asked."},{"p":"“No. That’s why I asked.”"},{"p":"She headed for the door."},{"p":"I followed her out into the heat. The asphalt smelled warm, and a little airplane was turning off the taxiway in the distance, its propeller a blurred disk. I could hear the engine but not the words Mara was saying to somebody behind us."},{"p":"“Imani?”"},{"p":"She stopped."},{"p":"“Do you ever get tired of being the one who asks questions?”"},{"p":"She looked at me for a long second. “Do you ever get tired of pretending you don’t have any?”"},{"p":"I laughed, but she didn’t."},{"p":"Then she shifted the chart to her other arm and walked toward her mother’s car."},{"p":"I stood by the curb with my backpack sliding off one shoulder. The zipper had come halfway open. A pencil fell out, struck the pavement, and rolled under the bench."},{"p":"I crouched to get it."},{"p":"From that low angle I could see beneath the bench and across the ramp fence, where the Skyhawk sat in the same parking spot as before. The red streamer was gone. I couldn’t tell from here whether the airplane had been cleared to fly, and for once I didn’t try to guess."}]},{"t":"Chapter Nineteen — A Lighter Hand","blocks":[{"p":"The airplane was back on the schedule Thursday afternoon. I knew because I’d checked the academy calendar before school, during lunch, and once in the library when I was supposed to be looking up the population of Delaware."},{"p":"Qualified maintenance personnel had evaluated the mark I’d found, and the school had documented the airplane’s return to service. Mara confirmed its maintenance status before we went anywhere near the ramp. I understood about half of the paperwork. The important part was that somebody qualified had looked, and the airplane was back."},{"p":"I wanted to ask whether finding the mark had made me a good pilot. I managed not to."},{"p":"When Dad dropped me off, the heat came through the open car door like somebody had aimed a hair dryer at my face. He had a library book balanced on the dashboard because the passenger seat was full of reusable grocery bags."},{"p":"“Call when you’re done,” he said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“And listen to your instructor.”"},{"p":"“Dad.”"},{"p":"“What? She might have useful information about airplanes.”"},{"p":"I closed the door before he could say anything else. He rolled down his window anyway."},{"p":"“Also, Delaware has just under a million people, depending on the year.”"},{"p":"I stared at him."},{"p":"He grinned and drove away."},{"p":"Mara was standing near the flight-school office, looking at a weather display on a tablet. Her hair was pulled into its usual low ponytail, but a few strands had escaped and stuck to her cheek."},{"p":"“Before you ask,” she said, “we’re not promising a flight until we finish the briefing.”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to ask.”"},{"p":"“You were walking toward me with your mouth open.”"},{"p":"“I could’ve been saying hello.”"},{"p":"“Hello, Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“Hello.”"},{"p":"She turned the tablet so I could see it. The symbols and numbers looked familiar enough to make me confident for about three seconds."},{"p":"“Tell me what you notice.”"},{"p":"“It’s hot.”"},{"p":"“Very good. We’re in Virginia in summer. Anything else?”"},{"p":"I read the wind information, then the visibility. I had to stop and remember which numbers meant what. Mara waited. She was getting better at waiting without making it look like a test, which somehow made it more of a test."},{"p":"“The wind isn’t very strong,” I said. “But it’s not straight down the runway.”"},{"p":"“Right. What would you want to know before deciding what that means for us?”"},{"p":"I almost said that it wouldn’t matter much. I’d seen other airplanes take off in worse-looking weather."},{"p":"Instead I looked at the runway diagram posted on the wall. “Which runway we’re using. And how much of the wind is across it.”"},{"p":"Mara tapped the screen once. “Good place to start.”"},{"p":"We discussed the conditions, the plan, and what we’d do if they changed. She checked the airplane’s documents and status. I read the checklist with her, slowly enough that she didn’t have to tell me to slow down."},{"p":"At the left wing I caught myself looking at the place where the mark had been. The metal was clean now. I didn’t know whether I was relieved or disappointed. Part of me had liked being the person who’d noticed something important."},{"p":"Mara followed my eyes."},{"p":"“It’s okay to be glad the airplane’s back,” she said."},{"p":"“I am.”"},{"p":"“You don’t have to find something wrong every time to prove you were paying attention.”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t trying to.”"},{"p":"She gave me a look."},{"p":"“Not exactly,” I admitted."},{"p":"Inside the cockpit, the seat belt buckle was hot enough to make me switch hands. Mara helped me position the seat and verify that I could reach the controls without stretching. We went through the before-start checks, and I read each item from the printed list while she performed or verified the actions appropriate to her role."},{"p":"The engine shuddered to life. The vibration traveled through the seat and into my ribs, and I couldn’t help grinning."},{"p":"Mara handled the radio work and the taxi. She told me what she was doing, but I didn’t try to memorize every transmission. I watched the yellow line slide beneath the nose and the shadows of the wings move over the pavement."},{"p":"At the run-up area we completed the required checks. A bead of sweat crept down my back. I could feel my heartbeat in the headset cushions."},{"p":"“Ready?” Mara asked when we’d finished."},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"She glanced over. “That’s an answer to a different question. What do you understand about today’s lesson?”"},{"p":"I swallowed. “You’ll handle the takeoff and landing. Once we’re in the practice area, I get to work on straight-and-level flight and gentle turns. You can take the controls whenever you need to.”"},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"“If I don’t understand something, I say so.”"},{"p":"“Now we’re ready.”"},{"p":"The takeoff still startled me. I knew what was coming, but knowing and feeling were two different things. The wheels rolled faster; the runway edges blurred; then the hard bumps stopped, and the ground began to drop away."},{"p":"I watched a row of trees pass below us. Their shadows lay across a field like dark brushstrokes. Beyond them, the roads and rooftops were arranged in tidy little pieces, which was funny because traffic never felt tidy when you were sitting in it."},{"p":"Mara climbed to the altitude she’d planned and cleared the area before handing over control. We used the same positive exchange we’d practiced before. Her hands came away only after she knew I had the airplane."},{"p":"“Pick a point outside,” she said. “Keep the nose where it belongs relative to the horizon. Small corrections.”"},{"p":"I chose a pale gap between two clumps of trees far ahead. The airplane drifted a little, and I moved the yoke."},{"p":"Too much."},{"p":"The nose rose. I pushed. It dipped."},{"p":"My shoulders tightened."},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“What do you know?”"},{"p":"“That I’m doing it again.”"},{"p":"“Then what can you change?”"},{"p":"I loosened my grip. My fingers had been wrapped around the yoke as though it might escape."},{"p":"“Less,” I said."},{"p":"“Try it.”"},{"p":"For several seconds the airplane behaved, or I behaved, or both of us did. I wasn’t sure. Then I glanced down at the instruments, saw a number I didn’t like, and started another correction."},{"p":"Mara spoke before I finished it. “Look outside.”"},{"p":"I did. The horizon had hardly moved."},{"p":"“Oh.”"},{"p":"“That’s a useful word.”"},{"p":"We practiced again. She had me notice the picture through the windshield, then make a small input and wait long enough to see the result. The waiting was the hardest part. At home, when I pushed a button, I expected the thing to happen immediately. Unlike a video game, the airplane didn’t reward me for pressing more buttons. Every extra movement gave me something else to fix."},{"p":"After a while Mara asked for a gentle turn toward a distant landmark. I started turning and watched the wing lower. It felt like tipping a dinner plate with a full glass on it."},{"p":"“Don’t chase every little movement,” she said. “Set the attitude. Watch what happens.”"},{"p":"The turn wasn’t perfect. I wandered away from the altitude we wanted, and Mara helped me correct. Once, she took control to reposition us and then handed it back. I didn’t like losing the controls. I liked even less how relieved I felt when she did."},{"p":"But the next time she offered them, I took them."},{"p":"We were heading back when I noticed my right hand wasn’t aching anymore. I’d been squeezing the yoke so hard on my first lesson that my fingers had hurt for an hour afterward. Now they rested against it lightly."},{"p":"“Did you see that?” I asked."},{"p":"“What?”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t fighting it.”"},{"p":"“Not for that stretch.”"},{"p":"I turned toward her. “You could sound happier.”"},{"p":"“I’m very happy. I’m also watching where we’re going.”"},{"p":"That made me look outside again."},{"p":"On the way back, a little patch of bumpy air rocked us. My stomach rose toward my throat. Mara explained what we were feeling and kept the airplane steady. I watched her hands. She didn’t make a production out of any of it."},{"p":"After she landed, we taxied back and completed the shutdown checklist. The propeller slowed, flashed once in the sun, and stopped. The quiet inside the cockpit seemed too large for such a small airplane."},{"p":"Mara unfastened her belt and reached for her notebook."},{"p":"“Tell me one thing you did better.”"},{"p":"“Didn’t squeeze the yoke to death.”"},{"p":"She wrote something down. “One thing to work on.”"},{"p":"“Everything else?”"},{"p":"“Try again.”"},{"p":"I looked through the windshield at the painted line on the ramp. “I still look at the instruments too long when I’m nervous.”"},{"p":"She nodded. “That’s something we can work with.”"},{"p":"I waited for her to tell me I was a natural. She kept writing. Then she handed me the pencil."},{"p":"“Write that in your lesson notes before you forget it.”"},{"p":"The pencil was blunt. I wrote anyway."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty — The Parts You Can’t See","blocks":[{"p":"On Saturday, Beckett arrived with a shoebox full of junk."},{"p":"That’s what it looked like, anyway. There were cardboard strips, paper clips, a rubber band, a plastic ruler, and something that had once been part of a kitchen timer. He carried it into the classroom like it contained expensive scientific equipment."},{"p":"Rafe leaned over to inspect it."},{"p":"“Did your garage explode?”"},{"p":"“No.” Beckett put the box on the table. “I brought materials.”"},{"p":"“For what?”"},{"p":"“Building.”"},{"p":"“Building what?”"},{"p":"“I’ll tell you when Mara gets here.”"},{"p":"Rafe picked up the timer part. It made a little clicking noise."},{"p":"“Put that down.”"},{"p":"“Does it still work?”"},{"p":"“It worked before you touched it.”"},{"p":"I sat beside Imani, who had her notebook open to a page full of weather symbols. She was drawing tiny arrows beside the numbers."},{"p":"“Studying?” I asked."},{"p":"“Trying to remember which way the wind direction is reported.”"},{"p":"“From where it’s coming.”"},{"p":"“I know that part.” She covered the page with her hand. “I’m checking myself.”"},{"p":"I leaned back. “Okay.”"},{"p":"She glanced at me. “You’re allowed to help without acting like I asked for help.”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t acting like anything.”"},{"p":"“Sure.”"},{"p":"Mara came in carrying a box fan and a roll of masking tape. She set the fan on a chair and looked at Beckett’s supplies."},{"p":"“Looks like somebody planned ahead.”"},{"p":"Beckett sat straighter."},{"p":"Rafe pointed at the timer piece. “He says it’s a machine.”"},{"p":"“I said it was a part.”"},{"p":"“Same thing.”"},{"p":"“Not remotely.”"},{"p":"Mara plugged in the fan but left it off. “Today we’re making gliders. Not because paper gliders are miniature Cessnas. They’re not. We’re using them to make observations and ask better questions.”"},{"p":"She gave us a few rules: no throwing toward anyone’s face, no climbing on furniture to launch, and no claiming we’d discovered a new law of physics after one successful flight."},{"p":"Rafe raised his hand. “What about two successful flights?”"},{"p":"“Three, and I’ll notify the scientific community.”"},{"p":"We divided into pairs. Imani chose me before I had time to wonder whether she’d forgiven me for our argument outside the academy."},{"p":"“Don’t make it weird,” she said, dragging two sheets of paper toward us."},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to.”"},{"p":"“You were thinking about it.”"},{"p":"I folded the first glider the way I always had, sharp nose, narrow wings. Imani made one with broader wings and a flatter front. Mine looked faster. Hers looked like a paper envelope that had given up on being mail."},{"p":"We launched from a tape line on the floor. Mine went straight for about eight feet, dipped, and struck a chair leg. Hers floated farther but veered left before landing."},{"p":"“Mine would’ve gone farther without the chair,” I said."},{"p":"“Mine would’ve gone farther without the wall.”"},{"p":"We both looked at the wall. It was nowhere near her glider."},{"p":"“That doesn’t make sense,” I said."},{"p":"“Exactly.”"},{"p":"She wrote down what happened. I hadn’t written anything. When I reached for my glider to change the wings, she stopped me."},{"p":"“Wait. What are you changing?”"},{"p":"“The wings.”"},{"p":"“Both?”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Then how will you know which change helped?”"},{"p":"I stared at the paper airplane in my hand. Beckett had said almost the same thing weeks ago. I hadn’t liked it then, either."},{"p":"“Fine. One wing.”"},{"p":"“One thing,” she corrected. “Not necessarily one wing.”"},{"p":"I flattened the left crease slightly, measured it against the right, and folded it back. We launched again. The glider curved differently. Not better, exactly. Differently."},{"p":"For the next twenty minutes, we measured, guessed, tested, and argued over whether a launch counted if somebody’s sleeve brushed the paper. Rafe’s glider flew into the fan before Mara had switched it on. Beckett built something with a little movable tab at the back and refused to call it an elevator until Mara explained the comparison and its limits."},{"p":"Then she turned on the fan."},{"p":"The papers on our table fluttered. My first instinct was to hold the glider directly in front of it, nose pointing into the breeze. Imani moved to the side."},{"p":"“What are you doing?” I asked."},{"p":"“Seeing what happens when the airflow comes from another direction.”"},{"p":"“It’s going to blow sideways.”"},{"p":"“Probably.”"},{"p":"She held it out. The glider rolled off her fingers and struck the floor almost immediately."},{"p":"Rafe laughed. Imani picked it up and checked the folds."},{"p":"“That’s one result,” she said."},{"p":"She tried again with a different position. The second launch traveled farther, though it still curved."},{"p":"I watched the glider settle beneath a desk."},{"p":"“You’re not annoyed?” I asked."},{"p":"“About what?”"},{"p":"“That it didn’t work.”"},{"p":"“It did something.”"},{"p":"“Not what you wanted.”"},{"p":"She crouched to retrieve it. “I wanted to find out what it would do.”"},{"p":"I had an answer ready, but it wasn’t a good one. So I kept it to myself."},{"p":"Later, Mara gathered us around the table. She asked what we’d observed and which comparisons were unfair. Beckett talked about changing too many things at once. Rafe admitted he’d launched his glider harder whenever he thought it might lose. Imani showed her notes. I showed mine, which were mostly measurements and one angry-looking question mark."},{"p":"“Any conclusions?” Mara asked."},{"p":"I raised my hand, then lowered it."},{"p":"“Go ahead,” she said."},{"p":"“I thought a glider that looked faster would go farther. Mine didn’t.”"},{"p":"“What might explain that?”"},{"p":"“Lots of things. The folds. The way I threw it. The chair.”"},{"p":"“The chair seems to have made an impression.”"},{"p":"“On the airplane, too.”"},{"p":"A few people laughed."},{"p":"Mara nodded toward the box fan. “Air moving around an aircraft matters. So does its shape, its speed, and how it’s controlled. Our paper experiments are simple, but the habit of observing carefully carries over.”"},{"p":"When class ended, Beckett began collecting his supplies. I picked up a ruler from the floor and handed it to him."},{"p":"“Your movable tab worked pretty well,” I said."},{"p":"He looked at me as though I’d spoken in a different language."},{"p":"“Thanks.”"},{"p":"“I still think my first design looked better.”"},{"p":"“That’s because you’re wrong.”"},{"p":"“That’s because you don’t appreciate style.”"},{"p":"He smiled, just a little, and dropped the ruler into the shoebox."},{"p":"Outside, the air was thick and still. Imani stood by the curb waiting for her ride. She had one of the gliders tucked inside her notebook so it wouldn’t get crushed."},{"p":"“You kept that?” I asked."},{"p":"“Of course.”"},{"p":"“It crashed twice.”"},{"p":"“Three times.”"},{"p":"“Why keep it?”"},{"p":"She opened the notebook. On the wing she’d drawn a small pencil mark where she’d made an adjustment. Beside it she’d written the distance from each launch."},{"p":"“Because next week,” she said, “I won’t have to guess what I did.”"},{"p":"Her mother’s car pulled up. She slid the glider between the pages, waved, and climbed in."},{"p":"I watched the car leave, then dug into my backpack for the notes from my flight with Mara. The page had folded in half. The pencil marks were smudged where my sweaty hand had rested on them."},{"p":"I could still read the last line."},{"p":"*Look outside. Wait. Smaller corrections.*"},{"p":"I smoothed the paper against my knee and tucked it into the notebook instead of stuffing it loose into the bag."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-One — The Question on the Card","blocks":[{"p":"The little girl was holding her headset by the ear cups, with the cord hanging almost to her knees."},{"p":"She stood outside the academy classroom while the rest of us packed up. Her father was at the front desk, talking to somebody about a registration form. Every few seconds she looked through the glass door toward the ramp, then down at the headset, then back toward the airplanes. The cord had looped around one of her sneakers."},{"p":"“You’re going to trip,” I said."},{"p":"She looked at me without moving."},{"p":"“Your cord.” I pointed."},{"p":"She lifted one foot. The cord slid free. “I know.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"She stared at the headset. “Is it supposed to make that noise?”"},{"p":"“What noise?”"},{"p":"She pushed an ear cup against my face."},{"p":"The speaker crackled, then squealed loudly enough to make me jump."},{"p":"“That one,” she said."},{"p":"I had absolutely no idea. Unfortunately, she was looking at me the way I’d looked at Uncle Everett when he showed me the Tomcat photograph. Like I was somebody who ought to know."},{"p":"“Probably the plug,” I said."},{"p":"She looked at the cord. “What’s wrong with it?”"},{"p":"“Could be a loose connection.”"},{"p":"“Can you fix it?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Why not?”"},{"p":"“Because I don’t know what’s wrong with it.”"},{"p":"She considered this. “You just said it was the plug.”"},{"p":"I glanced toward the classroom. Mara was putting away the paper gliders. Beckett was arguing with Rafe about whether a glider that hit the fan counted as having flown. I could’ve called either one over. Beckett might know. Then I’d have to listen to him explain how he knew."},{"p":"“Actually,” I said, “I guessed.”"},{"p":"The girl gave me a disappointed look."},{"p":"“Sorry.”"},{"p":"She tried the headset again. It crackled."},{"p":"“Come on,” I said. “Let’s ask somebody who knows.”"},{"p":"Mara noticed the headset before we reached the desk. “Don’t plug that into anything else yet. We’ll have the staff check it.”"},{"p":"The girl frowned. “But I want to use it.”"},{"p":"“I know.” Mara crouched so they were eye to eye. “We’ll find you one that works properly.”"},{"p":"“I brought this one from home.”"},{"p":"“Then we’ll keep it safe for you until someone can inspect it.”"},{"p":"The girl hugged the headset against her shirt. Her father joined us, thanked Mara, and walked with her to the waiting area. She glanced back at me once, still unconvinced I knew much of anything."},{"p":"Fair enough."},{"p":"Mara stood and watched them go. “You handled that well.”"},{"p":"“I told her it was the plug.”"},{"p":"“Was it?”"},{"p":"“I don’t know.”"},{"p":"“Then what did you handle well?”"},{"p":"I watched Beckett trying to retrieve a paper glider from behind the cabinet. Rafe was offering advice that mostly consisted of telling him to reach farther."},{"p":"“I stopped guessing.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded once. No speech. No dramatic pause. She just handed me a stack of laminated cards and asked me to put them in the supply drawer."},{"p":"The top card showed a Cessna’s instrument panel, with blank spaces for the instrument names. I could fill in most of them now, though I still sometimes mixed up what the vertical speed indicator told me with what I thought I could feel through the seat."},{"p":"I slid the cards into the drawer."},{"p":"“Lincoln,” Mara said."},{"p":"I turned."},{"p":"“Your mother emailed about next Thursday. She said you might have a schedule conflict.”"},{"p":"I knew exactly which conflict she meant. The last baseball game of the summer was Thursday afternoon. Coach had sent three reminders about it, each one with more exclamation marks than the last."},{"p":"“It’s not a big deal,” I said."},{"p":"“Which part isn’t?”"},{"p":"“The game.”"},{"p":"Mara studied me. “Are you sure?”"},{"p":"I shoved my hands into my pockets. “I can miss one.”"},{"p":"“You can. But you don’t have to decide in front of me.”"},{"p":"I wanted to say I’d already decided. But our team had finally started playing like we knew one another, and I hadn’t dropped a ball in weeks. I hated that both things were true."},{"p":"“I’ll ask my parents,” I said."},{"p":"“Good plan.”"},{"p":"On the way out, I found Beckett by the vending machine, studying a packet of crackers that had caught on the metal spiral instead of falling."},{"p":"“Don’t hit it,” I said."},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to.”"},{"p":"He pressed his forehead against the glass."},{"p":"“You were thinking about it.”"},{"p":"“I’m thinking about whether the machine owes me crackers.”"},{"p":"“It does.”"},{"p":"“See? We agree on something.”"},{"p":"He walked off to find somebody with a key. I stayed beside the machine, looking at the crackers hanging above the empty tray. They were right there, almost close enough to reach, which somehow made it worse."},{"p":"Dad picked me up ten minutes late. He apologized before I’d even opened the door."},{"p":"“Someone returned forty-three library books at closing,” he said. “Forty-three. I counted.”"},{"p":"“Maybe they really like reading.”"},{"p":"“Maybe they really like not returning books on time.”"},{"p":"I buckled in. The seat belt had been sitting in the sun and felt warm against my neck."},{"p":"Dad pulled away from the curb. “Mara says next Thursday might not work.”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“You’ve got the game.”"},{"p":"“I could skip it.”"},{"p":"He kept his eyes on the road. “You could.”"},{"p":"“It’s just baseball.”"},{"p":"“You’ve been playing with those kids since April.”"},{"p":"“It’s not like we’re winning a championship.”"},{"p":"“That’s not what I said.”"},{"p":"At the traffic light, he glanced over. “Do you want to go?”"},{"p":"I picked at the corner of my academy badge. The plastic had started peeling away from the paper inside."},{"p":"“I don’t know.”"},{"p":"Dad turned onto the main road. “That’s allowed.”"},{"p":"I waited for him to tell me what to do. He didn’t."},{"p":"A ladder was strapped to the pickup ahead of us. One orange strap slapped against the metal every few seconds. I watched it until the light changed."},{"p":"“Can I move the lesson?” I asked."},{"p":"“We can ask.”"},{"p":"“What if there’s no other opening?”"},{"p":"“Then you make a choice.”"},{"p":"I leaned my head against the window. The glass was hot."},{"p":"For months I’d been desperate for somebody to say yes to flying. Now I had a lesson on the calendar, and I was trying to figure out how not to go."},{"p":"Dad switched on the turn signal."},{"p":"“Can I tell you something?” he asked."},{"p":"“Are you going to tell me what to choose?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Then okay.”"},{"p":"He smiled at the road. “Your coach called last week. Said you’ve been paying attention in the outfield.”"},{"p":"“Mostly.”"},{"p":"“He sounded impressed.”"},{"p":"I looked at him. “Why didn’t you tell me?”"},{"p":"“Because I figured you already knew you were paying attention.”"},{"p":"“That doesn’t mean I knew he noticed.”"},{"p":"Dad shrugged. “Fair point.”"},{"p":"I sat up straighter. “What else did he say?”"},{"p":"“He asked whether you were coming Thursday.”"},{"p":"“That’s it?”"},{"p":"“That’s it.”"},{"p":"I turned the badge over in my fingers. The plastic made a faint clicking sound against my thumbnail."},{"p":"“Can we email Mara tonight?” I asked."},{"p":"“Sure.”"},{"p":"“Not to cancel. Just to see.”"},{"p":"“Just to see,” Dad said."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-Two — The Small Correction","blocks":[{"p":"The flight lesson moved to Saturday morning after another student rescheduled. Mom made me promise not to treat that as proof the universe existed to arrange things for me."},{"p":"I said I wouldn’t."},{"p":"She looked unconvinced."},{"p":"Thursday’s baseball game went eleven innings, which seemed unfair because it was supposed to be seven. We lost anyway. I caught a fly ball near the fence, struck out once, and got a double because the right fielder slipped on the grass. Coach shook everybody’s hand afterward, even the kid who’d spent the last inning arguing with the umpire about a ball that was very clearly a strike."},{"p":"On Saturday, the airport was already hot by eight."},{"p":"A line of small clouds sat low over the horizon, and the pavement smelled like yesterday’s rain. I found Mara outside the flight-school office with the airplane’s paperwork tucked beneath one arm."},{"p":"“Morning,” she said. “What do you want to accomplish today?”"},{"p":"“Fly better.”"},{"p":"“That’s not very specific.”"},{"p":"“Make smaller corrections. Look outside. Don’t stare at the instruments.”"},{"p":"“Better. Anything else?”"},{"p":"I rubbed the back of my neck. “Ask when I don’t understand.”"},{"p":"“Good.” She handed me the checklist. “Then let’s start on the ground.”"},{"p":"We walked to the Cessna together. Its white paint looked almost blue in the shade, though the sun had already found the top of the wing. A faint smear of insect marks ran along the leading edge. I touched the checklist against my leg as we approached."},{"p":"Mara didn’t quiz me at every step. Sometimes she waited while I found the right line. Sometimes she pointed out something I had walked past too quickly. Once I had to go back and check a latch because I’d looked at it without actually testing it."},{"p":"“Easy to do,” she said."},{"p":"“That doesn’t make it good.”"},{"p":"“No. It makes it something to work on.”"},{"p":"We finished the inspection. Mara went over the reported and forecast weather, including wind and visibility, and explained why the conditions were suitable for our lesson. I listened while she briefed the flight and made sure I understood what we’d practice. When she asked if I had questions, I asked two. One was about how much pressure I should use on the yoke. The other was whether we’d have enough time to repeat a maneuver if I messed it up."},{"p":"“We’ll decide as we go,” she said. “The airplane doesn’t care what you hoped to finish today.”"},{"p":"“Neither does my math teacher.”"},{"p":"“That might be the first accurate comparison you’ve made all summer.”"},{"p":"Inside the cockpit, the seat felt familiar and strange at the same time. The shoulder belt caught against my shirt. I adjusted it, checked the lap belt, and put on my headset. Mara settled into the other seat and helped me review the controls and our division of responsibilities."},{"p":"The engine started under her supervision. The vibration traveled through the seat frame and into my knees. Outside, a man in a reflective vest pushed a baggage cart across the ramp, moving slowly in the heat."},{"p":"Mara checked for traffic, announced our movements over the radio, and taxied while I followed the signs and markings we’d discussed. At Hampton Roads Executive, there was no tower to clear us onto the runway. Mara finished the checks, looked for traffic again, and announced our departure before we entered. I didn’t pretend I could remember every switch and instrument yet."},{"p":"When we took off, the runway dropped away beneath us and the rooftops beyond the airport began to flatten into a patchwork of gray and green. My stomach lifted, just a little. It still amazed me that a machine could do this without the world making a bigger fuss about it."},{"p":"Mara handled the departure. Once we were clear and in an appropriate area for the lesson, she asked me to take the controls."},{"p":"“Your controls,” Mara said."},{"p":"“My controls,” I replied."},{"p":"“Your controls,” she confirmed."},{"p":"I wrapped my fingers around the yoke. Not too tightly, I reminded myself. My knuckles were already whitening."},{"p":"“Look outside,” she said."},{"p":"I looked at the horizon. The airplane’s nose was a little higher than I’d expected. I made a small adjustment."},{"p":"Then another."},{"p":"Then I felt myself starting to chase the airplane again."},{"p":"I stopped moving the yoke."},{"p":"The Cessna kept flying."},{"p":"For a few seconds I did nothing except watch the horizon and feel the pressure in my fingertips. The airplane wandered slightly. I corrected it, smaller this time, and waited long enough to see what happened."},{"p":"“That’s closer,” Mara said."},{"p":"I wanted to grin. I didn’t, because I was concentrating, and also because grinning with the headset microphone against my mouth made me feel ridiculous."},{"p":"We practiced maintaining a steady attitude and making gentle turns with Mara guiding me through the steps. I still used too much pressure once. Another time I watched the instrument panel for so long that Mara reminded me to bring my eyes outside. I corrected, looked back, and tried again."},{"p":"A cloud shadow moved across a field below. The field went dark for a moment, then bright again. I could see a road running toward a cluster of buildings, tiny cars moving along it like beads on a string."},{"p":"“Where are you looking?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“Outside. At the road.”"},{"p":"“Good. Now bring your attention back to the horizon and your flight path.”"},{"p":"I did."},{"p":"The next turn wasn’t perfect. It wasn’t even especially pretty. But I noticed the airplane starting to bank more than I intended, eased the pressure, and let the correction take effect before making another."},{"p":"Mara glanced at me. “What changed?”"},{"p":"“I waited.”"},{"p":"“For what?”"},{"p":"“To see what the airplane did.”"},{"p":"“Keep doing that.”"},{"p":"I nodded."},{"p":"A few minutes later, Mara said, “My controls.”"},{"p":"“Your controls,” I replied."},{"p":"“My controls,” she confirmed. I rested my hands in my lap. They were damp. I’d been gripping the yoke harder than I thought."},{"p":"On the way back, Mara talked through the approach and landing while I watched. The runway appeared ahead, first as a pale strip between buildings and then as something much wider. I could feel the change in engine sound and the slight pressure of the descent. The wheels touched, rolled, and rattled over the pavement."},{"p":"I hadn’t done the landing. I hadn’t been close to doing it. But I’d watched more carefully than on my first flight, and this time I could connect some of what Mara said to what I saw."},{"p":"After shutdown, the cockpit went quiet enough for me to hear somebody laughing outside near the hangar."},{"p":"Mara removed her headset. “How do you think that went?”"},{"p":"“Better.”"},{"p":"“Which part?”"},{"p":"I thought about it. “I didn’t keep moving the yoke every time something looked different. And I remembered to look outside more.”"},{"p":"“What still needs work?”"},{"p":"“Pretty much everything.”"},{"p":"She smiled. “Specifics, Lincoln.”"},{"p":"I sighed. “My eyes kept dropping to the instruments. I gripped too hard. I rushed the first turn.”"},{"p":"She wrote something on the lesson sheet."},{"p":"“Those are useful observations.”"},{"p":"I leaned toward the page. “What are you writing?”"},{"p":"“Things to practice next time.”"},{"p":"“Can I see?”"},{"p":"She turned it so I could read. There were notes about visual references, control pressure, and taking time to recognize what the airplane was doing. Nothing about being a natural pilot. Nothing about being terrible, either."},{"p":"At the bottom she’d written: *Student recognized overcontrol and made smaller inputs with coaching.*"},{"p":"I read it twice."},{"p":"“That’s good?” I asked."},{"p":"“It’s progress.”"},{"p":"I looked out through the windshield at the empty stretch of ramp in front of us. Heat shimmered above the pavement. Somewhere beyond the hangars, another airplane was starting its engine."},{"p":"“Can I write something too?”"},{"p":"Mara handed me the pen."},{"p":"I added a line beneath her notes, trying to keep my handwriting small enough to fit."},{"p":"*I didn’t have to fix everything right away.*"},{"p":"She read it without commenting. Then she clipped the sheet back onto the board and opened her door. “Come on,” she said. “Let’s get this airplane secured.”"}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-Three — The Other Side of the Fence","blocks":[{"p":"The first thing I saw at the academy’s family introduction the following Saturday was a little boy wearing a headset upside down."},{"p":"The ear cups were balanced on top of his head like enormous black earmuffs. The microphone pointed at the ceiling. He was standing beside the classroom door, holding a folded sheet of paper with both hands."},{"p":"“Are you a pilot?” he asked me."},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"His face fell so quickly I almost changed my answer."},{"p":"“I’m learning,” I added."},{"p":"“Me too.”"},{"p":"He looked about nine, with a grass stain on one knee and a plastic airplane clipped to his backpack."},{"p":"“Your microphone goes by your mouth,” I told him, pointing."},{"p":"He pulled the headset down until the ear cups covered most of his ears. The microphone ended up somewhere near his nose."},{"p":"“Close enough?”"},{"p":"“It’s a start.”"},{"p":"Inside, the classroom smelled like dry-erase markers and somebody’s burnt toast. Rafe was leaning against the counter with a paper cup of water, and Beckett had spread three model airplanes across a table. Imani was labeling a chart with colored pencils. Mara stood near the whiteboard, checking names against a clipboard."},{"p":"The little boy followed me in."},{"p":"“This is Owen,” Mara said. “He’s visiting with his grandfather. They’re here for the family introduction this morning.”"},{"p":"Owen lifted his paper. “I have questions.”"},{"p":"“That’s usually how this works,” Mara said."},{"p":"He unfolded the sheet. There were at least twenty questions written in enormous handwriting. One of them had been crossed out so hard it had torn the paper."},{"p":"“What makes a plane stay up?” he asked."},{"p":"I opened my mouth. Lift. Airflow. Pressure. I knew pieces of the answer, and I could feel myself arranging them into a speech that would sound impressive."},{"p":"Then I remembered Mara asking me what I meant by *better*."},{"p":"“It’s a few things working together,” I said. “As the airplane moves through the air, its wings change the airflow and generate lift. The propeller provides thrust, and the pilot has to manage the airplane’s attitude and speed.”"},{"p":"Owen stared at me. “So the wings don’t just float?”"},{"p":"“Nope.”"},{"p":"“Good. Because that would’ve been weird.”"},{"p":"Beckett made a choking sound into his sleeve."},{"p":"Mara handed Owen a foam glider. “Why don’t you and Lincoln see what happens when you change the way you launch this? Stay inside the marked area.”"},{"p":"For the next ten minutes, Owen threw the glider nose-first into the carpet. Every time it hit, he announced that the airplane was broken. I showed him how to hold it level and give it a gentle push. The glider traveled across the room, wobbled, and landed beneath Rafe’s chair."},{"p":"“Whoa,” Owen said."},{"p":"“Try it.”"},{"p":"His first throw went sideways. His second went straight for Beckett’s models, which caused Beckett to make a noise like someone had stepped on his foot."},{"p":"The third one flew almost the whole length of the marked area."},{"p":"Owen jumped up and down. “I did it!”"},{"p":"“You did.”"},{"p":"He held out the glider. “You do it again.”"},{"p":"I nearly took it. A perfect throw would have looked impressive. Especially if Beckett was watching."},{"p":"Instead, I pushed it back toward him."},{"p":"“Your turn. See if you can get two in a row.”"},{"p":"He eyed me as though I’d slipped homework into the game."},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"The next flight nosed into a chair leg. He groaned. I recognized the sound. I’d made it myself in an actual airplane."},{"p":"“I used to move the controls too much,” I told him. “The airplane would start responding, and I’d already be making another correction.”"},{"p":"Owen considered this. “Did you crash?”"},{"p":"“No. My instructor was with me. She handled the parts I wasn’t ready to handle.”"},{"p":"“That’s good.”"},{"p":"“Very good.”"},{"p":"He picked up the glider and tried again."},{"p":"Later, after Owen and his grandfather left, Mara gathered us around the table. She asked what we’d learned during the summer. Rafe said that radio calls sounded a lot easier when someone else was making them. Imani said weather forecasts were useful precisely because they weren’t promises. Beckett said he had learned not to leave models where foam gliders could destroy them."},{"p":"“Nothing was destroyed,” I said."},{"p":"“One propeller was bent.”"},{"p":"“It’s made of plastic.”"},{"p":"“Plastic can bend, Lincoln.”"},{"p":"Mara looked at me. “And you?”"},{"p":"I looked at the ceiling fan. It wobbled on every turn. Earlier that summer, I probably would have tried to fix it."},{"p":"“That I can be nervous and still pay attention,” I said."},{"p":"Nobody laughed. Rafe nodded once, which was somehow more embarrassing."},{"p":"“And,” I added, “I’m still terrible at packing for the beach.”"},{"p":"“That,” Imani said, “was already established.”"},{"p":"Mara gave each of us our lesson notes. Mine made a surprisingly thick stack, with corrections on nearly every page. She reminded us that a summer of lessons wasn’t the same as qualifying to fly on our own. We had a lot left to learn. Nobody was getting a certificate or a set of keys today."},{"p":"I knew that already. It still stung a little."},{"p":"Outside, the heat rolled off the pavement. A Cessna taxied past at a careful pace. I watched its nosewheel track along the painted line until it disappeared behind a hangar."},{"p":"Beckett stood beside me with his models tucked under one arm."},{"p":"“You want to come over next week?” he asked. “I found plans for a glider that might actually fly straight.”"},{"p":"“Might?”"},{"p":"“It’s a design goal.”"},{"p":"I looked at him. He was waiting, but not staring at me, which made it easier to answer."},{"p":"“Sure. I can bring a notebook.”"},{"p":"“Bring a ruler too. Yours is longer.”"},{"p":"“You’ve been measuring my ruler?”"},{"p":"“I notice things.”"},{"p":"Imani came through the door carrying her chart tube. Rafe followed, pretending to give air traffic control instructions to a beetle on the sidewalk. It refused to follow them."},{"p":"For a minute, none of us hurried to leave."},{"p":"Beckett bumped my shoulder with his model box. “You coming?”"},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said, and followed them."},{"p":"The airplane was gone from sight, but I could still hear it somewhere beyond the hangars."},{"p":"I didn’t need to chase the sound."}]},{"t":"Epilogue — The Ball Above the Lights","blocks":[{"p":"By the time school started again, the outfield grass had turned patchy and the evenings arrived earlier. I was playing catch with Dad at the park near our house. He’d brought his old glove, the one with a loose lace that he promised to fix every spring."},{"p":"“Ready?” he called."},{"p":"“Ready.”"},{"p":"He threw the ball high, higher than he probably meant to. It climbed past the light pole and hung against a pale blue strip of sky."},{"p":"For a second I lost it."},{"p":"My first instinct was to run. I took two quick steps, then stopped and looked up again. The ball was drifting toward my left. I moved beneath it, lifted my glove, and waited."},{"p":"It landed with a sharp pop that stung my palm."},{"p":"Dad raised both arms. “There we go!”"},{"p":"“Your throw was terrible.”"},{"p":"“Your catch was excellent.”"},{"p":"“Both can be true.”"},{"p":"He laughed and held up his glove."},{"p":"Behind him, a small airplane crossed the sky, no bigger than a pencil mark from where we stood. I watched it until the trees hid it. I couldn’t tell where it had come from or where it was going, and I didn’t pretend I could."},{"p":"I threw the baseball back. Dad caught it against his chest, which wasn’t how gloves were supposed to work."},{"p":"“Again?” he asked."},{"p":"I looked toward the place where the airplane had disappeared. Then I turned back to him."},{"p":"“Again.”"}]},{"t":"Ground School Notes — The Things Lincoln Learned","blocks":[{"p":"Flying is exciting partly because there is so much to notice. These notes explain a few real aviation ideas from Lincoln’s summer. They aren’t instructions for flying an aircraft. Actual flight training requires a qualified instructor, an appropriate aircraft, and careful attention to the rules and conditions that apply."},{"h":"1. Four Forces, One Airplane"},{"p":"Four forces help explain airplane flight: **lift**, **weight**, **thrust**, and **drag**. Lift acts approximately perpendicular to the relative wind, while weight acts toward Earth’s center. Thrust pushes or pulls the airplane through the air; drag resists that motion. Their relative sizes and directions can change as the airplane climbs, descends, turns, or changes speed."},{"p":"A wing generates lift by changing the airflow around it, creating pressure differences and deflecting air. Its shape, angle of attack, and airspeed all matter. An engine doesn’t directly create the wing’s lift. In a typical propeller airplane, the engine turns the propeller, which produces thrust. Airspeed and angle of attack help determine the lift the wing produces."},{"p":"**Try this:** Draw an airplane flying straight and level. Add arrows for lift, weight, thrust, and drag. Then sketch it in a climb. How might the arrows change? Remember that lift acts roughly perpendicular to the relative wind, not necessarily straight up."},{"h":"2. Pitch, Roll, and Yaw"},{"p":"An airplane can rotate around three axes. **Pitch** moves the nose up or down, **roll** banks the wings, and **yaw** swings the nose left or right. In a conventional Cessna 172, the elevator, ailerons, and rudder primarily control pitch, roll, and yaw, respectively. The motions can affect one another."},{"p":"A pilot doesn’t usually move one control and forget about everything else. The airplane responds as a whole, and the pilot has to observe that response. This is one reason Lincoln’s habit of making rapid corrections caused trouble."},{"p":"**Try this:** Hold a model airplane or a folded paper airplane. Demonstrate pitch, roll, and yaw one at a time. Can you describe each motion without saying only “turn”?"},{"h":"3. Look Outside—and Check the Instruments"},{"p":"Pilots flying in visual conditions use outside references, including the horizon, to help judge an airplane’s attitude and flight path. Instruments provide important information too. A student who stares only at the panel can miss what is happening outside. A student who ignores the instruments can miss other important information. Learning how to divide attention is part of flight training."},{"p":"Lincoln’s instructor, Mara, coaches him to use a visual reference, make a small input, and give the airplane time to respond. She remains responsible for the training flight and takes the controls when necessary."},{"p":"**Try this:** Watch a bicycle rider from a safe place away from traffic. Notice how small steering changes help the rider stay on course. The comparison isn’t exact, but it can help explain why constant large corrections are rarely useful."},{"h":"4. Checklists Are for Everyone"},{"p":"Before flight, pilots follow procedures and use checklists to reduce the chance of overlooking important items. A checklist isn’t a sign that someone has a poor memory. It is a way to reduce the chance of missing a step, especially when distracted or in a hurry."},{"p":"If a student notices something unusual during a supervised preflight inspection, the right response is to tell the instructor. The pilot in command is responsible for determining that the airplane is in a condition for safe flight. That includes checking required documents and inspection status and involving qualified maintenance personnel when needed. On Lincoln’s supervised lessons, Mara is the pilot in command. A canceled flight may be disappointing, but a schedule is never a reason to ignore a possible safety problem."},{"p":"**Try this:** Make a checklist for preparing for a day at school, a sports practice, or a family outing. Have someone else test it. What did you forget? What can you remove because it doesn’t belong?"},{"h":"5. Weather Is Information, Not a Promise"},{"p":"Wind can change in direction and strength. Clouds, visibility, temperature, and other conditions also affect flight planning. Pilots use observations and forecasts, then keep evaluating conditions as they change. A forecast describes what is expected; it cannot guarantee exactly what the sky will do."},{"p":"A **crosswind** is the component of wind that blows across a runway rather than along it. Pilots learn special techniques for dealing with crosswinds, and instructors decide when conditions are appropriate for a student’s experience."},{"p":"**Try this:** On three different days, watch a flag or a tree from a safe location. Record the time and what you notice about the wind. Does it stay steady? Does it come in gusts? Compare your observations with a local weather report."},{"h":"6. A Route Is More Than a Straight Line"},{"p":"Aviation charts show airports, airspace, terrain information, and many other details. Flight planning considers much more than the shortest distance between two points. Pilots must account for airspace requirements, weather, aircraft performance, fuel, suitable airports, and other factors. Sometimes the sensible route is not the most direct one."},{"p":"**Try this:** On a map of your town, plan a bicycle route to a library or park. Compare the shortest route with one that has sidewalks, safe crossings, and less traffic. Explain why you chose the route you did. Don’t attempt the ride without permission and appropriate supervision."},{"h":"7. How Old Do You Have to Be?"},{"p":"In the United States, a person must generally be **at least sixteen** to solo a powered airplane and **at least seventeen** to be eligible for a private pilot certificate in an airplane. Other rules apply to gliders and balloons. Before soloing an airplane, a student also needs the applicable student pilot certificate, medical qualification, training, and instructor endorsements. Eligibility and medical requirements depend on the kind of operation; a student should confirm the current rules with a qualified instructor. A private pilot applicant must satisfy further eligibility, experience, and testing requirements. That doesn’t mean younger students can’t learn about aviation or take appropriate introductory lessons with an instructor. Lincoln is thirteen, so his flights in this book are supervised lessons—not solo flights."},{"p":"**Think about it:** What aviation skills can someone begin developing long before they are old enough to fly alone? Consider observation, weather awareness, communication, math, and good judgment."},{"h":"Lincoln’s Logbook Challenge"},{"p":"Pick one thing you want to learn—a sport, an instrument, a science project, or a new hobby. For one week, keep a short record of what you practiced, what went wrong, and what you will try differently next time. Don’t just write “good” or “bad.” Be specific. A useful record helps you notice progress that might otherwise be easy to miss."},{"p":"**Remember:** Aviation isn’t about pretending you know everything. It’s about learning carefully, asking questions, and making sound decisions with the information you have."},{"h":"AVIATION NOTE"},{"p":"Aviation procedures, regulations, publications, equipment, and airport information can change. This series is fiction and is not a substitute for flight instruction, current official publications, aircraft operating information, or the judgment of a qualified instructor or pilot in command. Readers interested in learning to fly should use current FAA materials and instruction appropriate to their training."},{"h":"HYPE TEEN FLIGHT ACADEMY"},{"p":"Hype Teen Flight Academy follows Lincoln Burgess and his friends as they grow from curious beginners into young aviators who understand that good flying begins long before takeoff. Across seven books, real aviation concepts become part of stories about friendship, judgment, preparation, setbacks, independence, leadership, and service."},{"p":"1. *Balance Before Control*"},{"p":"2. *Crosswind Decisions*"},{"p":"3. *Weather Minimums*"},{"p":"4. *Cross-Country*"},{"p":"5. *Solo*"},{"p":"6. *Pilot in Command*"},{"p":"7. *The Sky We Share*"}],"links":[{"a":"unit:flight","t":"How Airplanes Fly"},{"a":"unit:controls","t":"Flight Controls"},{"a":"unit:instruments","t":"Cockpit Instruments"},{"a":"unit:decisions","t":"Pilot Decisions"},{"a":"unit:weather","t":"Weather"},{"a":"unit:nav","t":"Navigation + Charts"}]}],"words":28806}); })();
